{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/1792210000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-review-r3.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md\ncat collab/1792220000000-architect-cursor-cell-numbering-revise3.report.md\ncat docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md'","aggregated_output":"# Cursor-cell numbering review r3\n\nReviewed HEAD `1bb2638cfcf00e509f34bf463e3083e51d593697`, including the uncommitted note and prototype. Phase 6a reference: `d77da9f04276fca34db6fe6c36bea38a576c09b2`. Only this findings file was edited. Review ran in the foreground, without agents. JVM commands used `mise exec -- java -Xmx1g @java.args clojure.main -` from the probe directory, with scripts supplied on stdin.\n\nThe previous speculative-run and install-child translation defects are fixed. The previous enumeration wording and size estimate are corrected. The work-bound defect is **not fixed**: dropping the polynomial claim is appropriate, but the replacement claim that budgets account for every allocated spelling byte is false. I found no accepted-state canonicality counterexample in the independent 5,000-state run. D6's canonicality argument nevertheless has concrete gaps, and F4's purported exact refusal coverage is incorrect.\n\nReferences below use `note` = `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md`, `probe` = `collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj`, and `rows` / `rows_test` = the phase 6a files named in the prompt.\n\n| Severity | Location | Finding and required correction |\n|---|---|---|\n| **HIGH** | note:369–395; probe:81–87, 112–125, 348, 355–403 | The claimed preallocation byte accounting is absent for cell spellings, row/tag/container overhead, ties, and repeated comparison encodings. `literal-bytes` itself allocates a complete CBOR encoding before checking the budget. Body-rank preparation and cell-incidence traversal are uncharged. Restore a conservative preallocation representation bound, charge encoding/comparison work consistently, and distinguish input-size allowance from cumulative allocation/work. |\n| **HIGH** | note:404–420, 456–468, 487–494; probe:270–272, 331–340 | The simultaneous induction does not cover the actual D6 procedure. Lemma A says key runs reaching conflicts do not choose; under D6 those runs recursively search. A trial starts with the same numbering and cell set, not strictly fewer unnumbered cells. Forcing a nested unordered element need not number a cell before recursive work begins. Supply a well-founded measure for key runs, synthetic component runs, forced traversal and recursive trials, and state the D6 version of lemma A. |\n| **MEDIUM** | note:157–177 | The exact-coverage paragraph overstates both the equal-body condition and the power of a single ordered anchor. Mixed-body components can refuse; an ordered anchor can leave a connected component unresolved. Retain the operational fixpoint/component predicate and replace the inaccurate structural paraphrase with qualified examples. |\n| **MEDIUM** | note:828–862; probe:414–433; rows:256, 289–292 | The replacement slice omits enforcement of `:cursor-cells` and the numbering traversal's `:depth` check. The prototype accepts 65, 1,000 and 20,000 cells under default budgets, and accepts them even with `:cursor-cells 1`. The deleted phase 6a procedure enforced these bounds. Add these gates explicitly to the replacement instructions and prototype; decoder input validation does not restore the lift-side gates. |\n| **MEDIUM** | note:896–899; rows_test:406–421 | “Same body” for the large-literal test is incompatible with the new totality assertion. The old `base` declares an unreachable cell and line 414 expects successful emit; discovery raises `{:missing [:x], :extra []}`. Rewrite that assertion as a totality-defect check, retaining successful large-literal tests for the reachable variants. |\n\n## Previous findings and independent reruns\n\n| Previous r2 finding | r3 status |\n|---|---|\n| HIGH: ambiguity over speculative key-run conflicts | **Fixed for D5.** D5, F4 and lemma D now distinguish speculative keys from required complete runs. The witness numbers and decodes in both modes. D6 needs its own proof wording, as above. |\n| HIGH: install-child refusal escapes decode | **Fixed.** D5 lift gives `[12 a 15]`; decode gives `:yin.k/undecodable :unresolved-cursor-cells`, path `[<root> 12 a 15]`. D6 numbers and decodes the child. |\n| HIGH: unjustified work bound | **Partially addressed, still blocking.** The polynomial guarantee is withdrawn and descriptor bytes disappear from provisional cell spellings. The replacement allocation guarantee is not implemented. |\n| MEDIUM: “never reads enumeration order” | **Fixed.** A2 now describes commitment/acceptance invariance and explicitly permits D4's equal-component order. |\n| MEDIUM: optimistic size/review estimate | **Size corrected.** The 330-line estimate is credible. A guaranteed implementation round plus review round remains a planning assumption, not evidence of reduced review cost. |\n\nThese targeted counterexamples were rerun independently in **both** modes:\n\n| Case | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `#{[X X] [Y Z]}` | X=1,Y=2,Z=3; root `65ba4530…` |\n| `{X X, Y Z}` | X=1,Y=2,Z=3; root `409548a7…` |\n| `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` | Complete numbering; root `18b70de6…` |\n| `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` | Complete numbering; root `92747c0f…` |\n| `[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]` | A=1,B=2; root `9600fe01…`; D5 work 50, D6 work 106/search 2 |\n| Store `'a #{X Y}, 'b X` | X=1,Y=2; 1 root over 24 renamed/shuffled copies; decode succeeds |\n| Both frame fixtures from r2 | y=1,x=2,test/cursor=3; 1 root over 24 copies each; decode succeeds |\n| Unreachable census cell | Totality defect with missing cell, not a refusal |\n| 3-cycle with D6 `:search 1` | `:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit [:search]` |\n\nThe five residue fixtures were also compared over 100 renamed/shuffled copies each:\n\n| Shape | D5 | D6 root | D6 trials/work |\n|---|---|---|---:|\n| 2-cycle | Refused | `0fe898cc…` | 2 / 77 |\n| 3-cycle | Refused | `0b181cd4…` | 3 / 144 |\n| Path `#{[A B] [B C]}` | Refused | `726a9fbb…` | 2 / 77 |\n| Loop plus 2-cycle | Refused after numbering L | `858a891e…` | 2 / 86 |\n| Triangle plus square | Refused | `d76d787a…` | 11 / 488 |\n\nAll D6 fixtures had one root. The first four decoded successfully. The seven-cell carrier hit the prototype decoder's retained **6a** recomputation limit `[:label-bytes]`; this is the documented probe limitation, not a D6 mismatch. D5 acceptance/refusal comparisons ignore source-name diagnostic text; renamed diagnostic lists are not expected to be byte-identical.\n\nThe independent generator was reconstructed around the prior review's ten incidence families, rather than using the author's recursive generator. Seed `179221`; 2–8 candidate cells with equal bodies; pair relations, overlapping groups, aliased maps, repeated/nested containers, reversed edges and ordered anchors. There were 250 install-child states. Each state was compared with **two independently renamed copies**, permuting every set, map and census. The renamer was implemented independently of `p/rename-and-shuffle`.\n\nAn initial run exposed duplicate decoded sets in my generator. I corrected that using a separate decoded-equality projection and reran the full 5,000 states. Only the corrected run is counted below:\n\n| Check | D5 | D6 |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| States | 5,000 | 5,000 |\n| Numbered | 2,883 | 5,000 |\n| Structurally refused | 2,117 | 0 |\n| Root or acceptance/refusal mismatches across copies | **0** | **0** |\n| Decode successes among first 100 states | 46 | 80 |\n| Decode resource limits in that subset | 11 | 20 |\n| Other decode failures | **0** | **0** |\n| D5-accepted roots changed by D6, across all 5,000 | **0** | **0** |\n\nNo general guarantee follows from these samples. The high refusal rate is deliberate tie-heavy incidence coverage, not an estimate for suspended production tasks.\n\n## Budget evidence\n\nI instrumented `cbor/encode` with an in-memory wrapper that counted calls and returned byte lengths, without changing source files:\n\n| Fixture | Charged work | Recorded local key bytes | All CBOR output bytes / calls |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| `#{10 equal-body cells}` | 105 | 1,850 | 6,667 / 79 |\n| `#{64 equal-body cells}` | 591 | 11,840 | 43,333 / 511 |\n| `#{128 equal-body cells}` | 1,167 | 23,680 | 86,789 / 1,023 |\n| Four cells/two bodies, descriptor 128 chars | 78 | 1,396 | 5,132 / 56 |\n| Same structure, descriptor 65,536 chars | 78 | 1,396 | 135,954 / 56 |\n\nThe set measurements are identical in D5 and D6. The descriptor fixture confirms the narrower rank-spelling benefit in both modes: local key bytes remain 1,396. It also demonstrates that rank construction's real encoded-byte cost still increases and is not charged to work. These totals are cumulative encoding bytes, **not** peak heap measurements.\n\nThe `#{64 cells}` result alone contradicts the note's one-unit-per-spelling-byte statement: 11,840 local key bytes versus 591 work units, before counting comparison encodings. Keys, ties and copied cursor-cell spellings are not literals, and therefore escape the byte charge. Repeated `bytes-key` calls also normalize/encode/materialize comparison vectors without charge. Memo keys and incidence scans have costs beyond the paid spellings.\n\nA 400,000-element literal in an ordered vector with a reachable cell numbers using 17 work units; it is not expanded into a local spelling, which is appropriate. Moving that value under an unordered element causes a work refusal. But `literal-bytes` obtains the full encoded bytes **before** that refusal: it cannot enforce a preallocation guarantee. A small work budget is a useful refusal test, not proof that every costly operation is charged before allocation.\n\nOrdered cell vectors charged 25, 79, 80, 1,015 and 20,015 work for 10, 64, 65, 1,000 and 20,000 cells. All accepted, including with `:cursor-cells 1`. Restore the census gate **before** ranks and memo preparation. Restore the structural depth gate and describe whether it applies to input structures or synthetic recursion; the latter needs its own well-foundedness argument.\n\nA fix should cache each paid encoded comparison key, conservatively check/count representation size before encoding, account for synthetic/tag/cell/tie overhead, and charge incidence/rank preparation. Alternatively narrow the budget promise and introduce an explicit conservative byte bound for intermediates. The current section promises more than the prototype delivers.\n\n## D6 proof and coverage\n\nLemma E's final minimum argument is sound **conditional on** each trial being canonical and complete: corresponding trial sets have the same multiset of faithful spellings; equal complete spellings exhibit isomorphic numberings; selecting the minimum is invariant. Choosing a different least conflicting group after a trial is also harmless if subsequent selection is already proved equivariant.\n\nThe missing part is the asserted induction establishing that condition. The actual trial at probe:338 is initialized with the parent's numbering. The forced element is encountered during traversal, not pre-numbered. In\n\n```clojure\n#{ #{[A B] [B A]}\n   #{[A C] [C A]} }\n```\n\nforcing one outer element enters a nested set whose edges are deferred. Merely forcing it does not immediately number A or B. Component items add synthetic sets and ordered group wrappers, so “strictly fewer nested unordered containers” must specify which structural measure excludes those wrappers and how it decreases. D6 numbered this example with 10 trials, work 393, 28 local runs and one root over 100 copies; D5 refused it. It is evidence of functioning recursion, not a justification of the stated induction.\n\nMoreover, D6 applies to speculative **key runs**: the r2 witness uses two search trials while computing keys even though D5 accepted the root without search. Lemma A iii's “does not choose, spells residue” argument therefore proves the D5 case only. The proof must distinguish modes and show nested key/component/trial transitions decrease a defined measure. I found no canonicality counterexample, but cannot certify lemma E as presently proved.\n\nF4's operational predicate about duplicate group membership in a residual sharing component is the useful exact characterization. Its following “exactly which structures” paragraph is not equivalent:\n\n1. `#{[A B] [A C]}`, with A at stream position 0 and B,C at position 1, refuses with cells `[A B C]`. The equal-body cells B,C are not both mentioned by either element; shared A has a different body. The claimed equal-body multi-cell-per-element pattern is too narrow.\n2. `[#{[A B] [A C] [B D] [C E] [D B] [E C]} A]`, all bodies equal, numbers A and then refuses at `[15 0]`, cells `[B C D E]`. An ordered reference to one vertex does **not** break the tie for the whole connected component. D6 numbers it using four trials, work 201, root `ba717359…`.\n3. The prose's “four distinct roots” for the seven-cell case is also stale: §6 itself reports one under the repaired permissive component entry. Such sampled equality is not proof of interchangeability.\n\nDo not infer a production-frequency or universal anchored-component claim from the cycle/path examples.\n\n## Rank order and section 8 audit\n\nFor the note's **own round-2 labels** `[:body [:stream …] position]`, rank replacement is order-preserving: distinct complete CBOR data encodings are prefix-free, comparison reaches the first differing rank position, and nonnegative CBOR integers sort in numeric order. The fixture pins and both-mode tests support that narrower claim.\n\nIt does **not** preserve the ordering of phase 6a's initial labels at rows:252–255. Those are:\n\n```clojure\n(cbor/encode [:body\n              (cbor/encode [:stream …])\n              (cbor/encode position)])\n```\n\nTheir embedded byte strings compare length headers before payload bytes. Direct structural labels compare the stream's contents instead. A body with stream name `:a`, descriptor `{:peer <128 x characters>}`, position 0, versus one named `:zz`, descriptor `{:peer \"\"}`, position 0, gives:\n\n- 6a label comparison: **+1** (a sorts after zz);\n- note's direct-label comparison: **−1**;\n- new ranks: `{:a 0, :z 1}`.\n\nThus answer to the requested 6a-order check is **no**. Clarify whether retaining 6a label order is intended. If so, compute ranks from exactly those label bytes, under an appropriate bound. If only round-2 discovery compatibility is intended, say so explicitly; discovery already changes other 6a roots, so this need not block adoption on its own.\n\nThe probe's rows and rows_test files match `srvm-phase6a` by SHA-1:\n`e02350337c1b48a8d02901e46499d4cd0ab90f19` and\n`d8aeed70b39a1f4770ba99fb699d06e6561a4128`.\nI inspected the named deletion/rewrite spans against those copies:\n\n- rows:212–230, 245–255, 257–284, 285–325, 326–354: the named label/check/encoding/position/refinement/search helpers and boundaries are accurate.\n- rows:185–189, 200–209, 358–372, 570–578: child numbering, budgets, emission entry points, recomputation and subsequent frame validation are accurately identified.\n- rows_test:171–178, 307–328, 331–338, 392–403, 406–421: names, fixture locations, removed pins and old budget checks are accurately identified.\n- The cut must preserve/reintroduce rows:256's census count gate and rows:291–292's depth gate, as above.\n- `descriptors` is listed both as extended and kept unchanged. Describe the base schema helper as reused with synthetic support added.\n- The 400,000-element test is not kept with the same body; its unreachable-census assertion must change. Successful reachable ordered-literal cases can remain.\n- Reusing rows' `bytes-key` needs care: it accepts already encoded bytes; the probe helper accepts a value and encodes it. They are not interchangeable without an encoding wrapper.\n\nPhase 6a row tests passed: **21 tests, 324 assertions, zero failures/errors**. This verifies the reference slice, not a production integration of discovery. No Node or Dart execution was performed.\n\n## Simplicity and recommendation\n\nD6 is **not materially simpler than 6a**, honestly. It removes iterative label growth/refinement but retains recursive search, now combined with alias-preserving local spelling, deferred worklists, incidence components, adoption, context-sensitive memoization, forced traversal and resource accounting. It has not reintroduced the same refinement algorithm; it has reintroduced much of the search/proof burden while adding different bookkeeping.\n\nThe probe's lines 67–449 contain 337 nonblank, noncomment lines (including multiline docstrings). Lines 34–449 contain 361. Reuse in rows reduces that, but correct byte/census/depth accounting adds work back. The note's estimated 250–300 added/replacement and 135–150 deleted lines is broadly plausible; my retained planning range is **about 100–195 net added lines, excluding tests**, with the budget fixes still unimplemented. D5 removes roughly 25 lines of D6 but adds refusal diagnostics and decode translation; the net simplification is small.\n\nCompared with 6a, D6 trades growing-label failure for nested trial/context/memo/forcing and accounting failures. Its proofs must establish canonical keys, residual alias completeness, component swaps, equivariant trial selection and a valid recursion measure. “Five lemmas” is not a meaningful reduction in those obligations. It does have a measured advantage on the seven-cell fixture: 11 residue trials instead of hitting 6a's label bound.\n\n**Recommendation within the owner's discovery-order decision: choose D6 after the blocking fixes.** D5's structural rejection is broader than the prose admits, and rejects simple legal symmetric states that 6a handles. The small code saving does not outweigh the refusal semantics, decoder translation and coverage regression. D6 repairs that regression and passed this adversarial run, but adopt it for those concrete benefits, not on a claim of materially simpler implementation or proof. Neither variant is ready under the present accounting and proof text.\n\n## Reproducible independent generator\n\nThe following is the corrected stdin script used for the reported 5,000-state run. It uses the prototype only for schema descriptors, fixture construction, numbering/emission/decode and census collection; generation, decoded equality, shuffling and renaming are independent.\n\n```clojure\n(require '[probe.discovery :as p] '[yin.vm.ucf.rows :as r] '[yin.vm.ucf.rows-test :as t])\n(def rng (java.util.Random. 179221))\n(defn sh [xs] (let [a (java.util.ArrayList. (vec xs))] (java.util.Collections/shuffle a rng) (vec a)))\n(defn c [x] [:cursor-cell x])\n(defn v [xs] [:row (r/node :vec (vec xs))])\n(defn dv [x] (case (first x) :row (let [n (second x) ss (:slots n)] (case (:kind n) :vec (mapv dv ss) :set (set (map dv ss)) :map (into {} (map (fn [[a b]] [(dv a) (dv b)]) (partition 2 ss))))) x))\n(defn s [xs] [:row (r/node :set (second (reduce (fn [[seen out] x] (let [k (dv x)] (if (contains? seen k) [seen out] [(conj seen k) (conj out x)]))) [#{} []] xs)))])\n(defn m [ps] [:row (r/node :map (vec (mapcat identity ps)))])\n(def b (t/cursor-cell-body :s 0))\n(defn task [value] (t/task-node (mapv #(vector % b) (sort (p/reached value))) value))\n(defn rename2 [n bij]\n (letfn [(val2 [x] (case (first x) :cursor-cell (c (bij (second x))) :row [:row (go (second x))] x))\n         (go [n] (let [ss (:slots n) k (:kind n)]\n          (r/node k (case k\n           :set (sh (mapv val2 ss))\n           :map (vec (mapcat identity (sh (mapv (fn [[a z]] [(val2 a) (val2 z)]) (partition 2 ss)))))\n           :cursor-cells (vec (mapcat identity (sh (mapv (fn [[a z]] [(bij a) (go z)]) (partition 2 ss)))))\n           (mapv (fn [d x] (cond (p/ref? d) (go x) (p/refs? d) (mapv go x) (= :C d) (bij x) (p/v? d) (when x (val2 x)) :else x)) (p/descriptors k ss) ss)))))] (go n)))\n(defn gen [i]\n (let [n (+ 2 (.nextInt rng 7)) cs (vec (range n)) cc #(c (nth cs (.nextInt rng n)))\n       edge #(v [(cc) (cc)]) es (vec (repeatedly (+ 2 (.nextInt rng 9)) edge))\n       group #(s (repeatedly (+ 2 (.nextInt rng 4)) cc))\n       value (case (mod i 10)\n         0 (s es)\n         1 (v [(s es) (s (sh es))])\n         2 (s (repeatedly 4 group))\n         3 (v [(s es) (cc)])\n         4 (s (map #(v [% (group)]) es))\n         5 (m (mapv (fn [x] [(c x) (cc)]) cs))\n         6 (v [(s es) (s (map #(v (reverse (:slots (second %)))) es))])\n         7 (s (map #(m [[(cc) %] [[:lit :anchor] (group)]]) es))\n         8 (s [(v [(s es) (group)]) (v [(group) (s (sh es))])])\n         9 (v [(s es) (s (repeatedly 4 group)) (cc)]))\n       root (task value)]\n  (if (zero? (mod i 20)) (p/with-child (t/task-node [] [:lit nil]) root) root)))\n(def stats (atom {}))\n(def different (atom []))\n(defn norm [x] (if (keyword? x) x (if (:refused x) :refused x)))\n(doseq [i (range 5000)]\n (let [q (gen i) ids (p/all-census-cells q)\n       copies (vec (repeatedly 2 #(rename2 q (zipmap ids (sh (mapv (fn [j] (keyword \"fresh\" (str j))) (range (count ids))))))))]\n  (let [a (binding [p/*numbering-opts* {}] (p/do-root q)) z (binding [p/*numbering-opts* {:residue-search true}] (p/do-root q))] (when (and (keyword? a) (not= a z)) (swap! different conj i) (when (= 1 (count @different)) (println \"D5-D6-CHANGE\" i (pr-str q) a z))))\n  (doseq [mode [false true]]\n   (binding [p/*numbering-opts* {:residue-search mode}]\n    (let [outcomes (mapv p/do-root (cons q copies))]\n     (swap! stats update [mode (if (keyword? (first outcomes)) :numbered (norm (first outcomes)))] (fnil inc 0))\n     (when-not (apply = (map norm outcomes)) (println \"MISMATCH\" i mode (pr-str q) outcomes) (throw (ex-info \"mismatch\" {})))\n     (when (and (< i 100) (keyword? (first outcomes)))\n       (let [e (p/error #(p/decode* (p/emit* q)))]\n        (swap! stats update [mode :decode (if (nil? e) :ok (:yin.k/kind e))] (fnil inc 0))))))))\n (when (zero? (mod (inc i) 1000)) (println \"PROGRESS\" (inc i))))\n(println \"COUNTS\" @stats \"D5-D6-CHANGES\" (count @different))\n```\n\nVERDICT: CHANGES_REQUESTED\n\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-12 +07\n# Report: discovery-order numbering, round 4 (base design) — claude-fable-5-1\n\n## Summary for the owner\n\n**The rule.** A cursor cell is named by when a fixed walk over the task first meets it: the n-th cursor cell met is `cc-n`. The walk goes through the task in the carrier's table order, so everything is ordered except the members of guest sets and the entries of guest maps. Those are turned into sorted vectors first: each member gets a key, which is the member spelled out with already-numbered cursor cells by number and the others by their body, by how many times they occur in the task, and by the order they first appear inside the member. Members with equal keys are tied; they are set aside until the rest of the walk has numbered what it can, then keyed again. A tie that still remains is entered in any order if a cheap test shows that swapping its members changes nothing in the whole state; otherwise the lift refuses.\n\n**Example.** `[P Q P]` numbers `P = cc-1, Q = cc-2` whatever the bodies. In `{'a #{X Y}, 'b X}` with equal bodies, `X` occurs twice and `Y` once, so `Y` is entered first: `Y = cc-1, X = cc-2`, and the root id is the same one phase 6a computes.\n\n**What is refused, and why.** `#{[A B] [B A]}`: both pairs key alike and share both cells, so the test cannot prove the swap harmless (it is), and proving it would need a search; refused as `:unresolved-cursor-cells`, and a receiver that recomputes it refuses `:yin.k/undecodable` of the same kind. Same for `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` and a few shapes like them. The seven-cell triangle plus square is refused too, and that one genuinely needs a search: entering a triangle edge first or a square edge first gives different bytes. Over generated states the refusal rate is 19 of 2000 random values, 4 of 200 frame-bearing states, 2 of 100 with install children, and 168 of 1000 of the reviewer's deliberately tie-heavy states; of all refusals, 21 were truly order-dependent and the rest were harmless ties the test could not certify.\n\n**Size and difficulty against 6a, honestly.** The prototype's procedure is 296 non-blank lines (about 80 of them docstrings, trace and two diagnostic switches); 6a's refinement plus search plus label bound is 136. In `rows.cljc` expect about 200 lines added and 135 deleted. So it is not smaller in code. It is simpler in what has to be understood and proved: no search, no growing labels, one sort, one test, one page of proof (§2 of the note) with a plain termination measure; the definition can be stated in a paragraph, which 6a's could not.\n\n**Two things to decide.** (1) The key spells each cell's occurrence count besides its body and local index, one integer from one pass; it is what numbers `#{[A B] [B C]}` and `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]` without a search and what makes several fixtures land on 6a's own roots; dropping it is a one-line change. (2) The `:work` budget now also counts bytes, so its default becomes 4194304 (6a had 200000 positions plus 1048576 label bytes).\n\n## What was done\n\n- `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md` rewritten (745 lines). Normative §§1–7: lines 1–569; the readable core (rule §1, proof §2, coverage §3) is lines 1–315, 15 over the 300 target; §4 (6a cut list with `srvm-phase6a` line numbers), §5 (acceptance), §6 (amendments A1–A10) and §7 (owner questions) are implementer material. Appendices (non-normative): A prototype and measurements, B round 3's D4/D5/D6 as an extension not adopted (proof sketch, the review's gap, round-3 measurements), C portable ids, D history. The round-3 text stays on disk as `collab/1792220000000-numbering-note.partial-round4.md`.\n- `collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj` rewritten to the base design exactly (no components, no trials, no `:search`); round 3's prototype kept unchanged as `probe/discovery_r3.clj` and its log as `run-r3.log`. New in the probe: the `:cursor-cells` gate before any work, the `:depth` gate in the walk, charging of the representation bound before every comparison encoding (6a's `size`), occurrence counts, the tie test, the `:member` path step, exhaustive interchangeability classification of refused states (every listed order of every container when at most 720 orders, else 48 samples), the reviewer's incidence-family generator, a `:count-spelling false` switch for the measurement of question (1). `run.sh` uses `mise exec -- java -Xmx2g @java.args clojure.main …`. Run in the foreground; log in `collab/numbering-probe/run.log`.\n\n## Codex r3 findings, status\n\n| Finding | Status in round 4 |\n|---|---|\n| HIGH: byte accounting absent | Every CBOR encoding for a comparison goes through one charged function that spends 6a's representation bound before encoding (keys, rank labels, structural keys, set spellings); positions, cell scans, rank and count preparation, memo keys charged; literals never copied into spellings (by reference), never encoded in ordered positions. Default raised to 4194304. Measured: max 624926 over 3300 states; 544554 for the 65536-char descriptor (encoded bytes 132540, all charged first). The 400000-element literal: free in a vector (work 1273), `:resource-limit [:work]` in a set, before any encoding. |\n| HIGH: D6 induction incomplete | D6 and the component runs are gone from the base. One proof (§2): induction on (sub-term size, unnumbered cells); the only recursion is the key walk over a proper sub-term; each W3 pass or tie step numbers a cell or replaces queued members by proper sub-terms. D6's gap is recorded in appendix B. |\n| MEDIUM: coverage paragraph wrong | §3 states the exact predicate (a surviving group fails (i) incomplete key, (ii) shared within group, (iii) shared across groups) with the reviewer's mixed-body and anchored examples as refused fixtures and the exhaustive verdicts (both interchangeable; the seven-cell state not). |\n| MEDIUM: `:cursor-cells` and `:depth` gates dropped | Both in §1.5 and the probe: 2 cells under `{:cursor-cells 1}` and 65 under the default refuse `[:cursor-cells]`; `[[[[X]]]]` under `{:depth 3}` refuses `[:depth]`. §4 keeps 6a's `:256` and `:291-292`. |\n| MEDIUM: large-literal test vs totality | §4 rewrites `input-row-bodies-do-not-spend-label-bytes`: `base` asserts the totality defect `{:missing [:x] :extra []}` (no `:yin.k/status`); `reachable`/`custody` keep succeeding; plus the in-set refusal. |\n| \"D6 not materially simpler than 6a\" | Accepted; D6 not adopted. Honest size comparison above and in appendix A. |\n| rank order vs 6a's label order | Ranks are from the structural label `[:body [:stream …] position]`, bytewise; 6a's nested-byte-string label order is not preserved (noted in round 3; discovery already changes other roots). |\n\n## Probe results (round 4)\n\n- Invariance failures: **0** of 3300 states (each compared with itself renamed and with every set, map and census shuffled; refusals compared by verdict and path).\n- Decode: 0 failures; 2710 carriers decode and re-emit to their root; 397 stop at 6a's retained `[:label-bytes]` recompute inside the probe's decoder (probe limitation, as in round 3).\n- Numbered / refused: random 1981/19 (16 interchangeable-but-refused, 3 truly non-interchangeable), frame-bearing 196/4 (3/1), with children 98/2 (1/0/1 undecided), incidence families 832/168 (92/17/59 undecided). Reasons: every refusal was (ii) or (iii), none (i).\n- A more permissive test: host-order entry at every tie would have wrongly accepted the 21 non-interchangeable states (the justification for having a test). The round-3 component rule could recover at most 7 + 0 + 1 + 74 = 82 states (those failing only by sharing across containers). The occurrence count numbers 20 + 4 + 0 + 295 states that the literal \"body plus local index\" key would refuse; it loses none.\n- Fixture pins (appendix A table): `fab223…` (6a), `2d9873a8…` (review counterexample, now 6a's root), `07eda35d…`, `da69c679…`, `49d4478c…`, `387f294a…` and `be62ba43…` (codex A/B, 6a's roots), `18b70de6…`, `a850fce6…` (`[#{X Y} X]`, 6a's), `0193312…` (witness, 6a's), `726a9fbb…` (path), `d84480d4…`, `e67405ec…`, `6e7c7a6b…`, frame fixtures `0b9ef61e…`, `ff124bfc…`. Round-3 pins that moved: the review counterexample, `[#{X Y} X]`, codex A/B, the witness, `[#{#{X Y} #{Z W}} X]` (all because of the occurrence count); nothing is landed, so nothing breaks.\n- 6a's own tests in the probe project: 21 tests, 324 assertions, 0 failures.\n\n## Verdict\n\n**READY** for an implementation brief, conditional on the owner's two answers in §7 (occurrence count in the key; `:work` default). Remaining risks:\n\n1. The proof is hand-written and reviewed by nobody yet; the tie step (automorphism from index-matched bijections plus confinement) and the termination measure are the parts a reviewer should attack first. Codex's 5000-state generator from round 3 should be rerun against the base probe.\n2. Refusals in real programs: a guest set of pairs of cursors on one stream, with nothing else referencing them (the 2-cycle shape), refuses. No version-3 corpus exists to measure how often; the frame-bearing generator refused 2%.\n3. Representation-bound charging makes a literal of more than about 30000 scalars inside a guest set or map refuse `[:work]` under the default (6a's limit was about 8000). Ordered positions are unaffected.\n4. Portability of the key walk in `rows.cljc` (recursion depth bounded by `:depth`; `for` over possibly long sequences must become `mapv`/`reduce`; `bytes-key` signature differs between 6a and the probe), listed in §4.\n5. The probe decodes through 6a's decoder with its recompute budget retained, so 12% of the generated carriers were not decode-checked; the real codec has no such second recompute.\n# Cursor-cell numbering by discovery order (companion to the row carrier)\n\nStatus: **Adopted by the owner 2026-10-12 (replaces carrier §6's\nrefinement and search); not yet implemented.** Round 4 of the same day\n(Architect, claude-fable-5-1): the base design the owner asked for after\nthe third review (`collab/1792210000000-…review-r3…findings.md`), with\nthe round-3 variants moved to appendix B as an extension that is not\nadopted. Subordinate to\n[`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md) (the\ncarrier, body version 3) and\n[`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md);\nvocabulary is UCF §7.5.3 (\"Terminology: cursor, cursor cell, cursor-ref,\ncell\") exactly. Evidence is slice 6a's `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc`\nand `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc` (branch `srvm-phase6a` at\n`d77da9f0`) and the prototype of appendix A. §§1–6 are normative; the\nappendices are not.\n\n**The rule in one paragraph.** A cursor cell is named by the order in\nwhich a fixed walk over the task meets it: the n-th cursor cell met is\n`:yin.k/cc-n`, as a de Bruijn index names a binder by position. The walk\nis pre-order in the carrier's table order, so every position is ordered\nexcept the members of a guest set and the entries of a guest map. Those\nthe carrier *transforms* into ordered vectors before numbering: each\nmember is given a key, the spelling of the member in which cursor cells\nalready numbered appear by number and the others by body, occurrence\ncount and the order in which they first appear inside the member; the\nmembers are sorted by key. Members whose keys are equal are *tied*; they\nare postponed until the rest of the walk has numbered what it can, and\nre-keyed. A tie that survives is entered in any order if a cheap test\nproves its members interchangeable (swapping them leaves the whole state\nunchanged); otherwise the lift refuses. Guest programs are unrestricted:\nsets and maps may hold cursor-refs anywhere, including as map keys.\n\n**Example.** `[P Q P]` numbers `P = cc-1`, `Q = cc-2` whatever the bodies.\nIn `{'a #{X Y}, 'b X}` with equal bodies, the set is reached first; `X`\noccurs twice in the task and `Y` once, so their keys differ and `Y` is\nentered first: `Y = cc-1`, `X = cc-2` (the root is 6a's, `2d9873a8…`).\nIn `#{[A B] [B A]}` the two pairs have equal keys and share both cells;\nthe test cannot prove them interchangeable (they are) and the lift\nrefuses `:unresolved-cursor-cells`. In `#{[A B] [B C]}` the keys differ\n(`B` occurs twice) and the path numbers `A, B, C = cc-1, cc-2, cc-3`.\n\n## 1. The rule\n\n**Vocabulary.** An **item** is a row or a value. The **walk** is the\nprocedure of §1.2 over one item from a numbering; the **root walk** is\nover the `:task` row from the empty numbering and its numbers are the\noutput; a **key walk** is the same procedure over one member of a set or\nmap from the enclosing walk's numbering, run only to produce the member's\n**key**. A **member** is a `:set` element or a `:map` entry taken as the\nsynthetic ordered row `[:entry key value]`. A **tie group** is a run of\nmembers of one container with equal keys. A cursor cell's **body rank**\nis the index of its census body label `[:body [:stream identity\ndescriptor] position]` among the census's distinct labels in bytewise\norder; its **occurrence count** is the number of positions of the task\n(census and install children excluded) that hold it. Install children\nare numbered as their own tasks by the same rule, in any order.\n\n### 1.1 The transformation and the key\n\nWhen the walk reaches a `:set` or `:map` it replaces the container, for\nits own purposes, by the vector of its members in key order. The key of\na member is the **spelling** of that member produced by a key walk under\nthe numbering at that moment (every member of one container is keyed\nfrom that one numbering):\n\n- a cursor cell numbered by the enclosing walk spells `[:cursor-cell\n  [:n k]]` (root numbers) or as the enclosing key walk spelled it;\n- a cursor cell the key walk meets unnumbered receives a **local index**\n  `j` (the order of first meeting, counted from the enclosing walk's\n  local counter so a nested key walk never reuses an index of its\n  enclosing one) and spells `[:cursor-cell [:u j r m]]`, `r` its body\n  rank and `m` its occurrence count, all exact CBOR integers;\n- `[:lit d]` as itself, structural data as itself (by reference, never\n  copied), `[:row B]` expanded in place, the census as `[:cursor-cells]`,\n  an install child as `[:child]`, keyed structural rows in canonical key\n  order, `:window` in register order, and a nested `:set` or `:map` as\n  the bytewise sorted vector of its members' spellings in which a tie\n  group the key walk could not certify (§1.3) is spelled in place as\n  `[:tie K … K]`, its common key once per member.\n\nKeys are compared bytewise on canonical CBOR; the spelling is hash-free.\n`[X X]` keys `[:vec [u0 r m] [u0 r m]]` and `[Y Z]` keys\n`[:vec [u0 r m] [u1 r m']]`: the alias pattern inside a member is part\nof its key, and so is each cell's occurrence count, which separates\n`#{[A B] [B C]}` and `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]` without a search (appendix A).\n\n### 1.2 The walk\n\n**W1, order.** Depth-first, pre-order, from the walk's item. A row's\nslots in table order (`rows/row-table`, `rows.cljc:37-57`); ordered\nrepeated slots (`frames`, `:vec`, `:list`, `:k`) in order; keyed rows\nwith structural keys (`:bindings`, `:store`, `:parked-table`, the root's\n`installs` and `module-stores`) in the Jing canonical byte order of the\nkey; `:window` in ascending register order. The census is not entered;\nan install child's `:task` row is not entered. A `:set` or `:map` is\nentered as §1.1 orders it: members with a unique key in key order, an\nentry's key before its value; each tie group is **postponed** to the\nwalk's queue (queue order is canonical: containers in walk order, groups\nwithin one container in key order).\n\n**W2, meeting.** When the walk reaches a `[:cursor-cell C]` value or a\n`C` slot of a `:pending` row whose cursor cell has no number, the cell\nreceives the next number (the root walk counts from 1). A numbered cell\nis passed over. Every occurrence emits `[:cursor-cell :yin.k/cc-n]`; the\ncensus row stays the contents table.\n\n**W3, postponed ties.** After the pre-order walk, the queue is taken in\norder: each group's members are re-keyed under the current numbering and\ntreated as in W1 (unique keys entered in key order, surviving ties\nre-queued in order; a tie met inside an entered member joins the same\nqueue). Repeat while a pass numbers at least one cursor cell and groups\nremain. When a pass numbers nothing, the surviving groups go to the tie\ntest.\n\n### 1.3 The tie test\n\nA surviving group **passes** when all of (i) its common key is complete\n(contains no `[:tie …]`), (ii) the unnumbered cursor cells of its members\nare pairwise disjoint, and (iii) none of them occurs in a member of any\nother surviving group of the same walk. (At this point every unnumbered\ncell occurs only in postponed members, so (ii) and (iii) say: the cells\nof each member are confined to that member.) If every surviving group\npasses, all of them are entered, each group's members in the order the\nhost lists them (§2 shows the rows do not depend on it), and the walk\nreturns to W3. If any group fails, the **root walk refuses** (§1.4) and a\n**key walk stops**: its surviving groups are spelled as `[:tie …]`\nresidue and its key is incomplete. The test inside a key walk reads only\nthe member being keyed; it never reads the enclosing state.\n\nThis is the cheapest sound test: (i)–(iii) are what is needed for the\nindex-matched swap of two members to be an automorphism of the state;\nwhatever it refuses may still be interchangeable (§3 says what), but\nnothing it accepts can be host-order dependent.\n\n### 1.4 Refusal, and decode\n\nThe root walk refuses `:yin.k/non-portable` kind\n`:unresolved-cursor-cells`, `:yin.k/path` the row path of the container\nholding the first failing group in queue order (a container nested under\na member of a set or map has the enclosing container's path followed by\n`:member`, since no index into an unordered container is canonical; for\nan install child the path is prefixed `[12 <install name> …]`), and\n`:yin.k/cursor-cells` the sorted unnumbered cursor cells of the failing\ngroups' members. The verdict and the path are functions of the state up\nto renaming and host enumeration; the cell list names local source ids.\nThis is not a resource refusal and never `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`.\n\nThe receiver decodes, recomputes the numbering of the root and of every\ninstall child, re-emits and compares root ids\n(`:yin.k/undecodable :noncanonical-cursor-cells` on mismatch). In 6a's\n`decode*` the recomputation is the `emit` call (`rows.cljc:570-575`);\nthe **whole** of that call is wrapped so that only a lift-side\n`:yin.k/non-portable :unresolved-cursor-cells` (root or child) becomes\n`:yin.k/undecodable :unresolved-cursor-cells` with `:yin.k/path` prefixed\nby the carried root id (a child's path reads `[<root> 12 <name> …]`); a\nresource refusal propagates as itself; nothing else can arise there,\nsince step 5 has checked literals and markers. The validator order stays:\nnumbering (step 6) after step 5 and before `validate-frames`\n(`rows.cljc:576-578`; test `numbering-precedes-frame-defect`).\n\n### 1.5 Budgets and gates\n\n- `:cursor-cells`: the census size is charged first, before ranks,\n  counts or any walk (6a's gate at `rows.cljc:256`, kept).\n- `:depth`: the walk's nesting; a member step counts one level (6a's\n  gate at `rows.cljc:291-292`, kept, now in the walk).\n- `:work`: one unit per position visited (walk, key walk, cell scans,\n  count and rank preparation) or spelled, one unit per cell in a key\n  walk's memo key, and, **before every CBOR encoding made for a\n  comparison** (keys, rank labels, structural keys), the representation\n  bound of the value encoded, computed by reading it: 6a's `size` of\n  `check-label-bytes!` (`rows.cljc:218-229`), an upper bound of the\n  encoding's bytes. Spellings hold input literals by reference, so their\n  own size is their spelled positions. Hence every byte allocated for a\n  comparison is charged before the allocation, and the bytes alive at\n  any time are bounded by the budget plus the input. No polynomial bound\n  is claimed; the budget is the bound. Default `4194304`, the scale of\n  the deleted `:label-bytes` (appendix A: at most 624926 over 3300\n  generated states, 544554 with a 65536-character stream descriptor). A\n  literal of more than about 30000 scalars inside a guest set or map\n  exceeds it (6a allowed about 8000); in an ordered position a literal is\n  never spelled and costs one unit.\n- `:rows`, `:bytes`: unchanged. `:label-bytes` and `:search` are deleted.\n\nExceeding any budget is the resource refusal of carrier §10.10\n(`:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit [<key>]`), never a canonicality\nverdict, at lift and at decode alike.\n\n### 1.6 Totality\n\nThe root walk ends with an empty queue or a refusal. Before emission the\nimplementation asserts that the numbering is a bijection from the\ncensus's cursor cells onto `1 … n`; a violation (a census cell no walk\nreaches) is a defect, an exception without `:yin.k/status` carrying the\nmissing and extra cells, never a refusal and never a partially numbered\ncarrier. 6a's test `input-row-bodies-do-not-spend-label-bytes`\n(`rows_test.cljc:406-421`) declares exactly such a cell in `base` and\nexpects `emit` to succeed (`:414`); §4 rewrites it.\n\n## 2. Proof of canonicality\n\nCall two configurations (abstract task, numbering, queue) **isomorphic**\nwhen a bijection `π` of cursor cells carries one to the other up to the\nhost order of every set, map and census, with `N'(π(c)) = N(c)` and\nqueues corresponding group by group. **Theorem:** on isomorphic\nconfigurations the procedure refuses at corresponding points or emits\nbyte-identical rows. The proof is an induction on the measure\n`(size of the walk's item as a sub-term, unnumbered cells of the walk)`.\n\n*Sort.* The key of a member is a function of the member and the\nnumbering up to isomorphism. The key walk reads structure, numbers,\nranks (a function of the census up to isomorphism) and occurrence counts\n(an isomorphism invariant); it orders nested containers by the keys of\ntheir members, canonical by the induction (a member is a proper sub-term,\nso the measure decreases); its only other listed-order input is the\norder in which it enters an interchangeable group, harmless by the tie\nstep below; and what it cannot certify it spells as `[:tie K … K]`, a\nfunction of `K` and the group's size. Equal keys therefore mean\nisomorphic members when the key is complete (a complete spelling is\nfaithful: structure slot by slot, every cell by local index, rank and\ncount, so matching indices is a bijection of the members' cells under\nwhich the members agree).\n\n*Walk.* W1 and W2 read only table order and keys; W3 repeats W1 on the\nqueue in a canonical order. So the numbering before the tie test, the\nsurviving groups and their keys are functions of the configuration up to\nisomorphism, and so is the verdict of the test, whose inputs (key\ncompleteness, the sets of unnumbered cells of members) are structural.\n\n*Tie step.* Let `G` pass with members `e_1 … e_k`. Their keys are equal\nand complete, so for each pair there is an index-matched bijection\n`π_ij` of their unnumbered cells carrying `e_i` to `e_j`; the cells of\ndistinct members are disjoint (ii) and occur nowhere else in the state\n(iii), so the permutation composed from these bijections, extended by\nthe identity, is an automorphism of the whole state that permutes the\nmembers of `G` and fixes everything else. Entering `G` in two host orders\n`σ`, `σ'` gives configurations related by that automorphism: numbers\nagree by construction (the cells of the t-th entered member receive the\nsame block, by local index), structures agree, queues agree. The rest of\nthe procedure is equivariant by the theorem's induction, so the rows are\nthe same. Passing groups are mutually independent by (iii), so entering\nall of them before the next pass changes nothing.\n\n*Emission.* Cells are emitted by number only and every unordered slot is\nsorted by canonical bytes under the final numbering (carrier §5), so\nisomorphic final configurations emit the same rows. ∎\n\n*Termination.* There is no search and no re-entry: the only recursion is\nthe key walk, over a proper sub-term of its caller's item, so key walks\nnest at most as deep as the structure (`:depth`). Within one walk, every\nW3 pass except the last numbers a cell, and every tie step either numbers\na cell or replaces the queued members by proper sub-terms of themselves\n(members with no unnumbered cell are equal values, which the carrier\nrefuses as duplicates at §8 step 5). The measure decreases at each step\nand the budget bounds the count.\n\nConsequences: a function of structure, invariant under renaming and host\nenumeration (the theorem with `π` a renaming, a permutation of\ncontainers, or both); deterministic, so two lifts of one state are\nbyte-identical and the receiver's recomputation is well defined; sharing\nsurvives (one number per cell, every occurrence spells it); the\ncross-engine law of continuation §2.1 follows as before.\n\n## 3. What is refused\n\nRefused iff, when the root walk's last pass numbers nothing, some\nsurviving tie group fails §1.3. Concretely one of:\n\n- **(i) an incomplete key**: a tied member contains a container whose\n  own ties the key walk could not certify. `#{#{[A B] [B A]} #{[C D]\n  [D C]}}` (interchangeable; refused because equal residue keys need not\n  mean isomorphic members: `#{[A B] [B A]}` and `#{[A B] [C D]}` key\n  alike).\n- **(ii) two tied members share a cell**: the 2-cycle `#{[A B] [B A]}`,\n  the 3-cycle, the loop plus 2-cycle `#{[L L] [A B] [B A]}`\n  (`rows_test.cljc:312-329`, where `L` is separated by its key, `A B`\n  are not), the mixed-body `#{[A B] [A C]}` with `A` on another stream\n  position than `B, C` (all interchangeable and refused), and the\n  seven-cell triangle plus square (`rows_test.cljc:392-397`), which is\n  **not** interchangeable: a triangle edge entered first and a square\n  edge entered first give different rows, so no sound test can accept it\n  without a search.\n- **(iii) a tied member's cell occurs in a tied member of another\n  container**: `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`, `[#{#{X Y}} #{[X Z] [Y W]}]`, and the\n  reviewer's anchored state `[#{[A B] [A C] [B D] [C E] [D B] [E C]} A]`\n  (`A` is numbered by its ordered position, which does not break the tie\n  of the rest; all three interchangeable and refused).\n\nEqual bodies are not required (the mixed-body case) and an ordered anchor\ndoes not certify a whole component (the anchored case). 6a numbers the\n2-cycle, the 3-cycle, the loop plus 2-cycle, `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`, the\nmixed and the anchored states, and refuses the seven-cell state and the\ntwo-2-cycle state at `[:label-bytes]`. Discovery order numbers what 6a\nrefuses at `[:label-bytes]` when the keys separate the members, and\nnumbers the path and `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]`, which 6a also numbers.\n\nMeasured (appendix A; refusals classified by entering the ties in every\nlisted order of every container when there are at most 720 such orders,\nelse 48 sampled orders):\n\n| Generator | States | Numbered | Refused | of which interchangeable | not interchangeable | undecided |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| random values (1–6 cells, two bodies, nested sets, maps with cursor-ref keys, edge sets, sets of sets) | 2000 | 1981 | 19 | 16 | 3 | 0 |\n| frame-bearing (windows, K frames, environments, parked state) | 200 | 196 | 4 | 3 | 1 | 0 |\n| with install children | 100 | 98 | 2 | 1 | 0 | 1 |\n| reviewer's tie-heavy incidence families | 1000 | 832 | 168 | 92 | 17 | 59 |\n\nEvery refusal was (ii) or (iii); none was (i). Invariance failures and\ndecode failures: 0 of 3300. No production corpus exists yet (no\nversion-3 lift before 6b), so these are generator rates, not frequencies.\n\n**Fixtures.** Every state named in this section and in §1's example is a\nfixture of §5 (items 12–16), including the refused ones.\n\n## 4. Replacing 6a: deleted, rewritten, kept\n\nAgainst `srvm-phase6a` (`d77da9f0`), `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` and\n`test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc` (line numbers of those files).\n\n**rows.cljc, deleted.** `check-label-bytes!` (`:212-230`) as a bound on\nlabels (its `size` function, `:218-229`, survives as the representation\nbound of §1.5); inside `canonical-numbering` (`:236-355`): the label\nseeding (`:252-255`), `ce-slot`/`ce` (`:257-284`), `positions`\n(`:285-325`) except its depth gate, `classes` (`:326-330`), `refine`\n(`:331-337`), `search` (`:338-354`), the `checked-*` wrappers\n(`:245-251`); in `default-budget` (`:200-202`) the keys `:label-bytes`\nand `:search`.\n\n**rows.cljc, rewritten.**\n- `canonical-numbering` (`:236-355`), same signature and contract\n  (`task`, optional `budget` → `{source-id :yin.k/cc-n}`; empty census\n  → `{}`): the census gate (`:256`) first; body ranks and occurrence\n  counts; then the root walk of §1.2 with its queue, the tie test, the\n  refusal and the totality assertion, as in the prototype's sections\n  \"cell scans\", \"runs\", \"spelling\" and \"procedure\"\n  (`collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj:79-416`), charging as\n  §1.5 says. Portable `.cljc` per continuation §12: no `@#'` reflection;\n  `mapv`/`into`/`reduce`, not `for`, over sequences that may exceed 32\n  elements (the prototype's `group-failure` uses `for`: replace);\n  `(assoc {} …)` never `(assoc nil …)`; atoms are fine on all three\n  hosts. 6a's `bytes-key` (`:91-94`) takes encoded bytes; the prototype's\n  takes a value: keep 6a's and encode first.\n- `descriptors` (`:117-124`): reused, with the synthetic `:entry` row\n  added (`[:V :V]`).\n- `default-budget` (`:200-202`): `:work` default `4194304`.\n- `emit-node` (`:181-197`): thread a row path so a child's refusal\n  reports `[12 <install name> …]`.\n- `decode*` recompute (`:570-575`): the translation of §1.4 around the\n  whole `emit` call.\n- Namespace docstring (`:1-17`): \"canonical cursor-cell numbering\" →\n  \"discovery-order cursor-cell numbering (design note §1)\".\n\n**rows.cljc, kept unchanged.** `row-table`, `pending-table`, `node`,\n`refuse!`/`bad!`, the marker checks, `bytes-key`/`compare-bytes`/\n`byte-compare`/`sorted-bytes`, the census order (`:137-156`),\n`emit-slot`/`emit-node` apart from the path, `spend!` (`:205-209`),\n`emit-numbered` (`:358-363`), `emit` (`:366-372`), `structural?`,\n`literal?`, `check-abstract!`, all of `decode*` before `:570` and after\n`:575`, `decode`, `canonical-under`, `frame->rows`, `rows->frame`,\n`validate-frames`, and the `:bytes`, `:rows`, `:depth`, `:cursor-cells`\nkeys with their defaults.\n\n**rows_test.cljc, kept.** `task-node`, `cursor-cell-body`, `fixture`\n(`:16-37`), `portable-frame` and `bijection`, `row-ids-are-jing-default`,\n`replace-row`, `window-sorted-and-unique`,\n`canonical-under-is-renaming-invariant` (`:143-156`),\n`alias-topology-and-census` (`:159-168`), `gates-and-graph-before-hashes`,\n`numbering-precedes-frame-defect` (`:206-213`),\n`schema-and-literal-canonicality`, `child-scope-and-shared-child-dag`\n(`:240-249`), `pending-envelope-and-nil-literal-are-preserved`,\n`task-root-checks-carried-code-and-frame`, `reversed` and\n`unordered-containers-are-order-free` (`:285-304`),\n`census-is-numeric-from-ten-cursor-cells` (`:331-338`), the marker tests\n(`:341-381`), `cursor-cell-and-identity-shaped-literal-are-distinct`\n(`:384-389`), the fixture `seven-cursor-cell-triangle-square`\n(`:392-397`).\n\n**rows_test.cljc, rewritten.**\n- `refinement-search-on-symmetric-cursor-cells-is-bounded` (`:171-178`)\n  → `symmetric-cursor-cells-are-interchangeable`: the two assertions on\n  equal roots and two numbers stay; the `{:search 1}` assertions become\n  `{:work 1}` → `:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit [:work]`.\n- `canonical-roots-are-pinned` (`:307-328`): `fab223…` and `c4a7c7…`\n  stay; the loop plus 2-cycle block (`:312-328`) asserts the\n  `:unresolved-cursor-cells` refusal with path `[15]` and cells `[:a :b]`\n  for the task and the same verdict and path for `renamed`; the\n  `{:search 2}` assertion and the pin `55ff168e…` go.\n- `intermediate-label-bytes-are-bounded` (`:400-403`) →\n  `seven-cursor-cells-are-refused`: `:yin.k/non-portable\n  :unresolved-cursor-cells`, path `[15]`, cells `[:a … :g]`, and the\n  renamed and shuffled state gives the same verdict and path.\n- `input-row-bodies-do-not-spend-label-bytes` (`:406-421`) →\n  `large-literals-in-ordered-positions-are-free`: `base` (its census\n  cell `:x` is unreachable) now asserts the totality defect of §1.6 (an\n  exception whose data is `{:missing [:x] :extra []}` and has no\n  `:yin.k/status`) in place of `(is (nil? (error #(rows/emit base))))`\n  (`:414`); `reachable` and `custody` keep emitting and decoding to the\n  expected tasks (`:415-421`); add: the same 400000-element literal\n  inside a guest set refuses `:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit\n  [:work]` under the default budget.\n\n## 5. Acceptance for the rewritten 6a numbering\n\n12. `rows-test/discovery-order-numbering`: `canonical-numbering` returns\n    the maps of appendix A for `fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y`, the review\n    counterexample, `[x x y]`, `[x y y]`, `[#{X Y} X]`, the witness\n    `[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]`, `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]`, the path and the two\n    frame fixtures.\n13. `rows-test/occurrence-count-separates-members`: the review\n    counterexample numbers `Y cc-1, X cc-2`, pinned `2d9873a8…` (6a's\n    root); the path pinned `726a9fbb…`; `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]` pinned\n    `d84480d4…`; each equal after renaming plus reversal of every set.\n14. `rows-test/interchangeable-ties`: `#{X Y}`, `#{[X X] [Y Y]}`,\n    `{X 1, Y 1}`, `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` number without refusal; reversal and\n    renaming give equal roots; pins `07eda35d…`, `da69c679…`,\n    `49d4478c…`, `18b70de6…`.\n15. `rows-test/alias-pattern-is-part-of-the-key`: `#{[X X] [Y Z]}` and\n    `{X X, Y Z}` give one root for both enumerations and after renaming,\n    pinned `387f294a…` and `be62ba43…` (6a's roots); the witness numbers\n    `B cc-1, A cc-2`, pinned `0193312…` (6a's root), and decodes.\n16. `rows-test/unresolved-cursor-cells-refuse`: the 2-cycle, the\n    3-cycle, the loop plus 2-cycle, the seven-cell state,\n    `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`, the mixed-body, the anchored and the two-2-cycle\n    states refuse at `emit` with `:yin.k/non-portable`\n    `:unresolved-cursor-cells`, the paths and cells of appendix A, the\n    same verdict and path after renaming plus shuffling; a carrier of\n    each built with `emit-numbered` decodes to `:yin.k/undecodable\n    :unresolved-cursor-cells` with the path prefixed by the root, never\n    `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`; a parent whose install `'a` holds the\n    2-cycle refuses at `emit` with path `[12 a 15]` and its\n    `emit-numbered` carrier decodes to the `:yin.k/undecodable` kind with\n    path `[<root> 12 a 15]`.\n17. `rows-test/canonical-roots-are-pinned`: `fab223…` and `c4a7c7…`\n    unchanged; `a850fce6…` (`[#{X Y} X]`), `4993b5b1…`, `536c810b…`,\n    `e67405ec…` (`[#{#{X Y} #{Z W}} X]`), `6e7c7a6b…` (ten cells) added;\n    `55ff168e…` removed.\n18. `rows-test/counterexample-search`: a seeded generator as in appendix\n    A (at least 2000 random value states, 200 frame-bearing, 100 with\n    install children) in CI: every numbered state's carrier decodes and\n    re-emits to its root; renaming every cursor cell plus shuffling every\n    set, map and census gives an equal root, or the same refusal verdict\n    and path; refusals are counted and reported; the largest `:work` is\n    reported.\n19. `rows-test/numbering-is-total`: a census cell the walk does not reach\n    raises the totality defect, not a refusal, before any row is emitted.\n20. `numbering-precedes-frame-defect`, `alias-topology-and-census`,\n    `canonical-under-is-renaming-invariant`,\n    `unordered-containers-are-order-free`,\n    `child-scope-and-shared-child-dag` and\n    `census-is-numeric-from-ten-cursor-cells` pass unchanged; the\n    rewritten tests of §4 pass.\n21. `rows-test/gates-and-work`: 65 cells refuse `:resource-limit\n    [:cursor-cells]` under the default budget and 2 cells under\n    `{:cursor-cells 1}`; `[[[[X]]]]` under `{:depth 3}` refuses\n    `[:depth]`; a state whose bodies carry a 65536-character descriptor\n    numbers under the default budget; `{:work 1}` refuses `[:work]` at\n    `emit` and, for a valid carrier, at `decode`.\n22. Budgets and docs: `:label-bytes` and `:search` gone, `:work` default\n    `4194304`; A1–A9 of §6 applied; kondo clean; `bb test:sub yin.vm`\n    green on three hosts; Node log shows `Testing yin.vm.ucf.rows-test`.\n\n## 6. Amendments to the carrier and continuation documents\n\n**A1 — carrier §6, from \"The procedure.\" to the end of step 5.** Replace\nwith §1 above. Keep the section's first paragraph (the counterexample)\nand amend \"What is defined, and what is not claimed\" to: \"The canonical\nnumbering is defined as the output of the discovery-order procedure\nbelow, a function of the state up to renaming of cursor cells and host\nenumeration of guest sets, maps and the census (proof in\n`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md` §2). It is not the\nminimum of the root bytes over all bijections. The one choice the walk\ncannot make by itself, between tied members of one guest set or map that\nthe tie test cannot prove interchangeable, is refused\n(`:unresolved-cursor-cells`); the refusal certifies nothing about\ninterchangeability and can be narrowed later without changing any\nnumbering assigned today.\"\n\n**A2 — carrier §6 \"Validation\".** Replace the paragraph's last two\nsentences, from \"Every labeling that yields the selected commitment is\nthereby accepted, and no encounter-order tie-break ever decides validity\"\nto the end, with: \"Every labeling that yields the selected commitment is\nthereby accepted. The emitted commitment and the acceptance decision are\ninvariant under the host's enumeration order of every set, map and\ncensus (numbering note §2); the procedure's one use of host order,\nentering the members of a tie group proved interchangeable, cannot\nchange the rows, and a receiver never decides validity by its own\nenumeration order. This is a lower-side structural refusal, not a lift\nkind. A receiver whose work bound (§10.10) is exceeded before the\nprocedure completes refuses with the structured resource refusal before\nany attachment, never labels the state `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`, and\nnever accepts an unfinished check; a receiver that reaches the tie\nrefusal refuses `:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`.\"\n\n**A3 — carrier §5 preamble.** \"The class-erased skeletons of §6 are used\nonly inside the numbering procedure and never decide the order of an\nemitted row\" → \"The spellings (keys) of §6 are used only inside the\nnumbering procedure and never decide the order of an emitted row\".\n\n**A4 — carrier §8 step 6.** \"Recompute §6 under the work bound, for the\nroot and every install child, and compare (`:yin.k/undecodable`, kind\n`:noncanonical-cursor-cells`; kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells` if the tie\nrefusal is reached in the root or in a child, translated from the\nrecomputation's lift-side refusal of that kind only, its path prefixed\nby the carried root id; or the resource refusal of §10.10 if the bound\nis exceeded, which propagates untranslated).\" In the diagnostics\nparagraph after step 12 add `:unresolved-cursor-cells` to the\n`:yin.k/undecodable` kinds; \"the two kinds this carrier adds to UCF\n§7.5.4's set: `:byte-string` and `:metadata`\" → \"the three kinds …,\n`:metadata` and `:unresolved-cursor-cells`\"; in §11 \"two lift kinds and\ntwo `:yin.k/undecodable` kinds are added\" → \"three … and three …\".\n\n**A5 — carrier §10.** Answer 9: \"a renaming-invariant\nrefinement-and-search procedure\" → \"the discovery-order procedure, which\nis invariant under renaming and host enumeration\". Answer 10: \"a bound\non decoded bytes, rows, cursor cells, traversal depth, refinement work\nand search nodes for §6\" → \"a bound on decoded bytes, rows, cursor cells,\ntraversal depth and traversal-and-spelling work for §6 (its `:work` unit\ncounts positions and representation bytes, charged before any spelling\nis encoded)\"; \"never falls back to encounter order\" → \"never substitutes\nhost enumeration order for the canonical walk\". Proposals list: \"as\ndefined by its procedure\" → \"as defined by discovery order, and the\n`:unresolved-cursor-cells` refusal\".\n\n**A6 — carrier §3, identity rows.** After \"minted by the lift under §6's\ncanonical numbering\" add \"(the n-th cursor cell met in §6's canonical\nwalk)\".\n\n**A7 — continuation §2.2.** \"numbered by the draft's §6 procedure\" →\n\"numbered by discovery order (draft §6 as amended by\n`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md`)\".\n\n**A8 — continuation §5.5.** (i) \"… and the only other identity it\nconsumes is the stream marker in each cursor cell's initial label\n(`[:body <stream-marker-bytes> <position-bytes>]`, draft §6 step 1) and\nin cursor-cell bodies\" → \"… and the only other identity it consumes is\nthe stream marker in each cursor cell's body label (`[:body [:stream\nidentity descriptor] position]`, draft §6 as amended, spelled by rank\ninside keys) and in cursor-cell bodies\". (ii) \"Unordered containers\n(`:set`, `:map`, `:store`, `:bindings`, the parked table) are encoded by\nsorting their elements' encodings bytewise (draft §6 step 2), never by\nhost traversal order\" → \"Guest sets and maps are entered in the bytewise\norder of their members' keys, and `:store`, `:bindings` and the parked\ntable in the byte order of their structural keys (draft §6 W1), never in\nhost enumeration order\". (iii) \"never derived from traversal order\" →\n\"never derived from *host* traversal order (the canonical walk of draft\n§6 is a function of the state and is what the procedure reads)\".\n\n**A9 — continuation §8.1 acceptance.** Item 10 stands; append items\n12–22 of §5.\n\n**A10 — UCF §7.5.3.** No change.\n\n## 7. Owner questions\n\n1. The key spells each unnumbered cell's **occurrence count** besides its\n   body rank and local index (§1.1). This is one integer per cell from\n   one pass; it is what numbers the path and `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]` without a\n   search and what makes the review counterexample, `[#{X Y} X]`, the\n   codex A/B states and the witness reach 6a's own roots. Appendix A\n   measures its effect (295 of 1000 tie-heavy states, 20 of 2000 random\n   ones numbered only because of it; nothing lost). Keep, or drop to the\n   literal \"body plus local index\" (a one-line change, fewer states\n   numbered, different pins)?\n2. `:work` default `4194304`, replacing 6a's `200000` positions plus\n   `1048576` label bytes by one budget that counts both. Agreed?\n\n---\n\n# Appendices (non-normative)\n\n## A. Prototype and measurements (round 4)\n\n`collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj` (scratch project under\n`collab/`, never committed) implements §1 exactly: one walk procedure\nused for the root walk and for key walks, memoized on (member, numbering\nrestricted to the member's cells, local counter); the gates and the\ncharging of §1.5; the totality assertion; an emitter that numbers install\nchildren by the same procedure; a decode path that runs 6a's `decode*`\nwith its numbering check off and wraps the whole discovery recomputation\nin §1.4's translation. Two diagnostic switches: `:permissive` (a failing\ngroup is entered in listed order, one group at a time, instead of\nrefusing; used only to decide whether a refused state is in fact\ninterchangeable, by trying every listed order of every container when\nthere are at most 720 such orders, else 48 samples) and `:count-spelling\nfalse` (the occurrence count left out of the key). The round-3 prototype\nis kept unchanged as `probe/discovery_r3.clj` for appendix B. Run from\nthe probe directory (`run.sh`; JVM only; the 6a copies there match\n`srvm-phase6a` by SHA-1):\n\n```\nmise exec -- java -Xmx2g @java.args clojure.main -e \"(require 'probe.discovery) (probe.discovery/-main)\"\n```\n\nResults of 2026-10-12, round 4 (`collab/numbering-probe/run.log`; 6a's\nown tests pass unchanged in the same project, 21 tests, 324 assertions).\nRoots marked (6a) equal 6a's root for the same state.\n\n| State | Numbering | Root | `:work` |\n|---|---|---|---:|\n| `fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y` (`rows_test.cljc:28`) | `x cc-1, y cc-2` | `fab223…` (6a) | 37210 |\n| review counterexample `'a #{X Y}`, `'b X` | `y cc-1, x cc-2` | `2d9873a8…` (6a) | 3455 |\n| `#{X Y}` | `x cc-1, y cc-2` | `07eda35d…` (6a) | 3185 |\n| `[x x y]` / `[x y y]` (`:159`) | `x cc-1, y cc-2` | `4993b5b1…` / `536c810b…` (6a) | 1275 |\n| `#{[X X] [Y Y]}` / `{X 1, Y 1}` | `x cc-1, y cc-2` | `da69c679…` (6a) / `49d4478c…` (6a) | 6197 / 5107 |\n| codex A `#{[X X] [Y Z]}`, both enumerations | `y cc-1, z cc-2, x cc-3` | `387f294a…` (6a) | 6198 |\n| codex B `{X X, Y Z}` | `y cc-1, z cc-2, x cc-3` | `be62ba43…` (6a) | 6212 |\n| codex C `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}`, and renamed | first listed inner set `cc-1, cc-2` | `18b70de6…` (6a) | 13831 |\n| `[#{X Y} X]` | `y cc-1, x cc-2` | `a850fce6…` (6a) | 3189 |\n| `[#{#{X Y} #{Z W}} X]` | `y cc-1, x cc-2, z cc-3, w cc-4` | `e67405ec…` | 13835 |\n| witness `[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]` | `b cc-1, a cc-2` | `0193312…` (6a) | 16564 |\n| `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]` | `x cc-1, y cc-2, z cc-3` | `d84480d4…` | 4848 |\n| path `#{[A B] [B C]}` | `a cc-1, b cc-2, c cc-3` | `726a9fbb…` | 6199 |\n| ten cells in a vector (`:331`) | `cc-1 … cc-10` in order | `6e7c7a6b…` (6a) | 12413 |\n| frame fixture: wait window `x`, K-frame window `y`, environment `y`, parked K-frame window `#{x y}` | `y cc-1` (free environment, slot 5), `x cc-2` (parked table, slot 8), `:test/cursor cc-3`; decodes | `0b9ef61e…` | 3836 |\n| frame fixture: wait window `#{x y}`, parked K-frame window `y` | `y cc-1, x cc-2, :test/cursor cc-3`; decodes | `ff124bfc…` | 3122 |\n| parent with two installs sharing the review-counterexample child | child `y cc-1, x cc-2`; one child row shared; decodes | `09124ab9…` | — |\n| 400000-element literal beside a cell in a vector | numbers | `be7fc44e…` | 1273 |\n\nRefused (all `:yin.k/non-portable :unresolved-cursor-cells`; verdict\nand path equal over 24 renamings plus shuffles; \"interchangeable\" is the\nexhaustive permissive verdict):\n\n| State | Path | Cells | Reason | Interchangeable | 6a |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2-cycle `#{[A B] [B A]}` | `[15]` | `a b` | shared within group | yes | `0fe898cc…` |\n| 3-cycle | `[15]` | `a b c` | shared within group | yes | `0b181cd4…` |\n| loop plus 2-cycle (`:312`) | `[15]` | `a b` | shared within group | yes | `55ff168e…` |\n| seven-cell triangle plus square (`:392`) | `[15]` | `a … g` | shared within group | **no** | `[:label-bytes]` |\n| `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` | `[15 0]` | `x y` | shared across groups | yes | `92747c0f…` |\n| mixed bodies `#{[A B] [A C]}` | `[15]` | `a b c` | shared within group | yes | numbered |\n| anchored `[#{…} A]` | `[15 0]` | `b c d e` | shared across groups | yes | numbered |\n| `#{#{[A B] [B A]} #{[C D] [D C]}}` | `[15]` | `a b c d` | incomplete key | yes | `[:label-bytes]` |\n| `[#{#{X Y}} #{[X Z] [Y W]}]` | `[15 0 :member]` | `w x y z` | shared across groups | yes | numbered |\n| `#{[#{X Y} #{X Y}] [#{P Q} #{R S}]}` | `[15 :member 0]` | `x y` | shared across groups | yes | `[:label-bytes]` |\n| install child holding the 2-cycle | lift `[12 a 15]`; decode of an `emit-numbered` carrier: `:yin.k/undecodable`, path `[<root> 12 a 15]`, cells `[cc-1 cc-2]` | | | | |\n\nGates: two cells under `{:cursor-cells 1}` and 65 cells under the\ndefault budget refuse `[:cursor-cells]`; `[[[[X]]]]` under `{:depth 3}`\nrefuses `[:depth]`; `{:work 1}` refuses `[:work]` at lift and, for a\nvalid carrier, at decode; the 400000-element literal inside a set refuses\n`[:work]`; an unreachable census cell is the defect\n`{:missing [:x], :extra []}`. The 65536-character descriptor state\nnumbers at `:work` 544554 (21290 at 128 characters; encoded comparison\nbytes 132540 against 1718, all charged before encoding).\n\nRandom search (seeded; each state compared with itself renamed and\nshuffled, and decoded through 6a's decoder, whose own retained\nrecomputation budget stops 397 of the carriers at `[:label-bytes]`: a\nprobe limitation, not a failure):\n\n| Generator | Numbered | Refused (interch. / not / undecided) | Only across groups | Count gain | Invariance fail | Decode ok / fail | max `:work` |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| random values, 2000 | 1981 | 19 (16 / 3 / 0) | 7 | 20 | 0 | 1789 / 0 | 624926 |\n| frame-bearing, 200 | 196 | 4 (3 / 1 / 0) | 0 | 4 | 0 | 179 / 0 | 202939 |\n| with children, 100 | 98 | 2 (1 / 0 / 1) | 1 | 0 | 0 | 75 / 0 | 434068 |\n| incidence families, 1000 | 832 | 168 (92 / 17 / 59) | 74 | 295 | 0 | 667 / 0 | 453009 |\n\n\"Only across groups\" counts refusals whose every failing group failed\nonly clause (iii): an upper bound on what the component rule of appendix\nB could recover. \"Count gain\" counts states numbered only because the\nkey carries the occurrence count; the reverse count is 0 everywhere. A\ntest that simply entered ties in host order would have accepted the 21\nstates classified not interchangeable, with host-order-dependent rows:\nthat is why a sound test is needed at all.\n\nSize: the prototype's procedure region (`discovery.clj:79-416`) is 296\nnon-blank lines, of which roughly 80 are docstrings, trace and the two\ndiagnostic switches; 6a's `canonical-numbering` plus\n`check-label-bytes!` is 136 non-blank lines. In `rows.cljc` the\nreplacement reuses `descriptors`, `bytes-key`, `compare-bytes`, `spend!`,\n`refuse!`, the `size` bound and `emit-node`; expect about 200 added or\nreplacement lines and about 135 deleted, excluding tests.\n\n## B. Round 3's variants: components (D4), conflicts (D5) and the residue search (D6). Optional extension, not adopted\n\nRound 3 (`collab/1792220000000-numbering-note.partial-round4.md`, 980\nlines, and `probe/discovery_r3.clj`) resolved surviving ties differently:\nlink tied members that share an unnumbered cell into **components**;\nencode each component by a local run over a synthetic\n`[:component g₁ i₁ g₂ i₂ …]` row; enter the components of least\nencoding; a component holding two members of one group is a **conflict**,\nrefused (D5) or resolved by **trials** (D6): one local run per member of\nthe duplicated group with that member entered first, the least complete\nspelling adopted, charged to `:search`. The base design of §1 keeps none\nof it: no component runs, no trials, no `:search` budget. What the\nextension would add over the base: `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` and the other\n\"shared across groups\" refusals (at most 82 of 3300 generated states,\nappendix A) via components, and with D6 every state of appendix A's\nrefused table, including the seven-cell state (11 trials) and 6a's own\nroots on the 2-cycle and 3-cycle.\n\n*Proof sketch (round 3, lemmas C and E).* Equal complete component\nencodings are faithful spellings and exhibit a bijection under which the\ncomponents are the same structure group by group; since the cells of\ndistinct components are disjoint and confined, the block swap is an\nautomorphism, so entering a run of equal encodings in host order gives\nthe same rows. For D6, the multiset of trial spellings is canonical if\neach trial is, and equal least spellings give isomorphic numberings. The\nthird review found the induction incomplete: a trial starts from the\nsame numbering and the same cells as its parent, the forced member need\nnot number a cell before recursive work begins, and key runs under D6\nsearch instead of spelling residue; a well-founded measure covering key\nruns, synthetic component runs, forced traversal and recursive trials\nwas never supplied. That, with the reviewer's judgement that D6 is not\nmaterially simpler than 6a, is why it is not adopted.\n\n*Measurements (round 3, `collab/numbering-probe/run-r3.log`).* Under D6:\n2000 random value states, 200 frame-bearing and 100 with children all\nnumbered, 0 invariance failures, 0 decode failures, at most 13 trials\nand 1470 `:work` units (round-3 accounting, positions only); the 2-cycle\nand 3-cycle reached 6a's roots (`0fe898cc…`, `0b181cd4…`), the loop plus\n2-cycle `858a891e…`, the seven-cell state `d76d787a…` in 11 trials and\n488 units; the reviewer's independent 5000-state run found no\ncanonicality counterexample in either mode and 2117 D5 refusals.\n\n## C. Why not portable ids minted in the VM\n\nThe owner asked whether a cursor cell could carry a portable id from\ncreation. Every candidate (branch `srvm-phase6a`,\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc`: the task's id counter `gen-id` `:227-230`\nand `gensym` `:2312-2318`; the creation sequence of `:stream/cursor`\nexecutions, `handle-cursor-unminted` `:557-568`, minted at `:2595-2602`;\na hash of occurrence and counter; a random id) names history, not\nstructure. Two tasks with structurally equal snapshots (two cursor cells\nwith equal bodies at equal positions, equal counters) carry different\nid sets when one created and dropped a third cursor and the other\nconsumed an unrelated fresh name; the cross-engine law would then make\nan execution trace part of the frame's identity, and lower would have to\nkeep a second global name beside its private resource entry. Discovery\norder gives the identity a structural name instead. Recommended against.\n\n## D. History\n\nRound 1 (2026-10-12): discovery order proposed; the orbit lemma was\nunsound (`#{[X X] [Y Z]}`) and nested ties were never processed\n(`#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}`). Round 2: alias-carrying keys, the fixpoint, the\ncomponent rule; the review found speculative key-run conflicts refusing\nwrongly and an install child's refusal escaping decode. Round 3: key\nruns never refuse, the child translation, rank spelling, the `:work`\nbudget, D6; the review found the byte accounting unimplemented, the D6\ninduction incomplete, the coverage paragraph wrong, the `:cursor-cells`\nand `:depth` gates dropped and the large-literal test incompatible with\ntotality. Round 4 (this text): the base design, with every one of those\nfindings addressed in §§1–4 and the variants moved here.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
