{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md | head -430'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Cursor-cell numbering by discovery order (companion to the row carrier)\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: **Adopted by the owner 2026-10-12 (replaces carrier §6's\n     4\trefinement and search); not yet implemented.** Round 4 of the same day\n     5\t(Architect, claude-fable-5-1): the base design the owner asked for after\n     6\tthe third review (`collab/1792210000000-…review-r3…findings.md`), with\n     7\tthe round-3 variants moved to appendix B as an extension that is not\n     8\tadopted. Subordinate to\n     9\t[`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md) (the\n    10\tcarrier, body version 3) and\n    11\t[`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md);\n    12\tvocabulary is UCF §7.5.3 (\"Terminology: cursor, cursor cell, cursor-ref,\n    13\tcell\") exactly. Evidence is slice 6a's `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc`\n    14\tand `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc` (branch `srvm-phase6a` at\n    15\t`d77da9f0`) and the prototype of appendix A. §§1–6 are normative; the\n    16\tappendices are not.\n    17\t\n    18\t**The rule in one paragraph.** A cursor cell is named by the order in\n    19\twhich a fixed walk over the task meets it: the n-th cursor cell met is\n    20\t`:yin.k/cc-n`, as a de Bruijn index names a binder by position. The walk\n    21\tis pre-order in the carrier's table order, so every position is ordered\n    22\texcept the members of a guest set and the entries of a guest map. Those\n    23\tthe carrier *transforms* into ordered vectors before numbering: each\n    24\tmember is given a key, the spelling of the member in which cursor cells\n    25\talready numbered appear by number and the others by body, occurrence\n    26\tcount and the order in which they first appear inside the member; the\n    27\tmembers are sorted by key. Members whose keys are equal are *tied*; they\n    28\tare postponed until the rest of the walk has numbered what it can, and\n    29\tre-keyed. A tie that survives is entered in any order if a cheap test\n    30\tproves its members interchangeable (swapping them leaves the whole state\n    31\tunchanged); otherwise the lift refuses. Guest programs are unrestricted:\n    32\tsets and maps may hold cursor-refs anywhere, including as map keys.\n    33\t\n    34\t**Example.** `[P Q P]` numbers `P = cc-1`, `Q = cc-2` whatever the bodies.\n    35\tIn `{'a #{X Y}, 'b X}` with equal bodies, the set is reached first; `X`\n    36\toccurs twice in the task and `Y` once, so their keys differ and `Y` is\n    37\tentered first: `Y = cc-1`, `X = cc-2` (the root is 6a's, `2d9873a8…`).\n    38\tIn `#{[A B] [B A]}` the two pairs have equal keys and share both cells;\n    39\tthe test cannot prove them interchangeable (they are) and the lift\n    40\trefuses `:unresolved-cursor-cells`. In `#{[A B] [B C]}` the keys differ\n    41\t(`B` occurs twice) and the path numbers `A, B, C = cc-1, cc-2, cc-3`.\n    42\t\n    43\t## 1. The rule\n    44\t\n    45\t**Vocabulary.** An **item** is a row or a value. The **walk** is the\n    46\tprocedure of §1.2 over one item from a numbering; the **root walk** is\n    47\tover the `:task` row from the empty numbering and its numbers are the\n    48\toutput; a **key walk** is the same procedure over one member of a set or\n    49\tmap from the enclosing walk's numbering, run only to produce the member's\n    50\t**key**. A **member** is a `:set` element or a `:map` entry taken as the\n    51\tsynthetic ordered row `[:entry key value]`. A **tie group** is a run of\n    52\tmembers of one container with equal keys. A cursor cell's **body rank**\n    53\tis the index of its census body label `[:body [:stream identity\n    54\tdescriptor] position]` among the census's distinct labels in bytewise\n    55\torder; its **occurrence count** is the number of positions of the task\n    56\t(census and install children excluded) that hold it. Install children\n    57\tare numbered as their own tasks by the same rule, in any order.\n    58\t\n    59\t### 1.1 The transformation and the key\n    60\t\n    61\tWhen the walk reaches a `:set` or `:map` it replaces the container, for\n    62\tits own purposes, by the vector of its members in key order. The key of\n    63\ta member is the **spelling** of that member produced by a key walk under\n    64\tthe numbering at that moment (every member of one container is keyed\n    65\tfrom that one numbering):\n    66\t\n    67\t- a cursor cell numbered by the enclosing walk spells `[:cursor-cell\n    68\t  [:n k]]` (root numbers) or as the enclosing key walk spelled it;\n    69\t- a cursor cell the key walk meets unnumbered receives a **local index**\n    70\t  `j` (the order of first meeting, counted from the enclosing walk's\n    71\t  local counter so a nested key walk never reuses an index of its\n    72\t  enclosing one) and spells `[:cursor-cell [:u j r m]]`, `r` its body\n    73\t  rank and `m` its occurrence count, all exact CBOR integers;\n    74\t- `[:lit d]` as itself, structural data as itself (by reference, never\n    75\t  copied), `[:row B]` expanded in place, the census as `[:cursor-cells]`,\n    76\t  an install child as `[:child]`, keyed structural rows in canonical key\n    77\t  order, `:window` in register order, and a nested `:set` or `:map` as\n    78\t  the bytewise sorted vector of its members' spellings in which a tie\n    79\t  group the key walk could not certify (§1.3) is spelled in place as\n    80\t  `[:tie K … K]`, its common key once per member.\n    81\t\n    82\tKeys are compared bytewise on canonical CBOR; the spelling is hash-free.\n    83\t`[X X]` keys `[:vec [u0 r m] [u0 r m]]` and `[Y Z]` keys\n    84\t`[:vec [u0 r m] [u1 r m']]`: the alias pattern inside a member is part\n    85\tof its key, and so is each cell's occurrence count, which separates\n    86\t`#{[A B] [B C]}` and `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]` without a search (appendix A).\n    87\t\n    88\t### 1.2 The walk\n    89\t\n    90\t**W1, order.** Depth-first, pre-order, from the walk's item. A row's\n    91\tslots in table order (`rows/row-table`, `rows.cljc:37-57`); ordered\n    92\trepeated slots (`frames`, `:vec`, `:list`, `:k`) in order; keyed rows\n    93\twith structural keys (`:bindings`, `:store`, `:parked-table`, the root's\n    94\t`installs` and `module-stores`) in the Jing canonical byte order of the\n    95\tkey; `:window` in ascending register order. The census is not entered;\n    96\tan install child's `:task` row is not entered. A `:set` or `:map` is\n    97\tentered as §1.1 orders it: members with a unique key in key order, an\n    98\tentry's key before its value; each tie group is **postponed** to the\n    99\twalk's queue (queue order is canonical: containers in walk order, groups\n   100\twithin one container in key order).\n   101\t\n   102\t**W2, meeting.** When the walk reaches a `[:cursor-cell C]` value or a\n   103\t`C` slot of a `:pending` row whose cursor cell has no number, the cell\n   104\treceives the next number (the root walk counts from 1). A numbered cell\n   105\tis passed over. Every occurrence emits `[:cursor-cell :yin.k/cc-n]`; the\n   106\tcensus row stays the contents table.\n   107\t\n   108\t**W3, postponed ties.** After the pre-order walk, the queue is taken in\n   109\torder: each group's members are re-keyed under the current numbering and\n   110\ttreated as in W1 (unique keys entered in key order, surviving ties\n   111\tre-queued in order; a tie met inside an entered member joins the same\n   112\tqueue). Repeat while a pass numbers at least one cursor cell and groups\n   113\tremain. When a pass numbers nothing, the surviving groups go to the tie\n   114\ttest.\n   115\t\n   116\t### 1.3 The tie test\n   117\t\n   118\tA surviving group **passes** when all of (i) its common key is complete\n   119\t(contains no `[:tie …]`), (ii) the unnumbered cursor cells of its members\n   120\tare pairwise disjoint, and (iii) none of them occurs in a member of any\n   121\tother surviving group of the same walk. (At this point every unnumbered\n   122\tcell occurs only in postponed members, so (ii) and (iii) say: the cells\n   123\tof each member are confined to that member.) If every surviving group\n   124\tpasses, all of them are entered, each group's members in the order the\n   125\thost lists them (§2 shows the rows do not depend on it), and the walk\n   126\treturns to W3. If any group fails, the **root walk refuses** (§1.4) and a\n   127\t**key walk stops**: its surviving groups are spelled as `[:tie …]`\n   128\tresidue and its key is incomplete. The test inside a key walk reads only\n   129\tthe member being keyed; it never reads the enclosing state.\n   130\t\n   131\tThis is the cheapest sound test: (i)–(iii) are what is needed for the\n   132\tindex-matched swap of two members to be an automorphism of the state;\n   133\twhatever it refuses may still be interchangeable (§3 says what), but\n   134\tnothing it accepts can be host-order dependent.\n   135\t\n   136\t### 1.4 Refusal, and decode\n   137\t\n   138\tThe root walk refuses `:yin.k/non-portable` kind\n   139\t`:unresolved-cursor-cells`, `:yin.k/path` the row path of the container\n   140\tholding the first failing group in queue order (a container nested under\n   141\ta member of a set or map has the enclosing container's path followed by\n   142\t`:member`, since no index into an unordered container is canonical; for\n   143\tan install child the path is prefixed `[12 <install name> …]`), and\n   144\t`:yin.k/cursor-cells` the sorted unnumbered cursor cells of the failing\n   145\tgroups' members. The verdict and the path are functions of the state up\n   146\tto renaming and host enumeration; the cell list names local source ids.\n   147\tThis is not a resource refusal and never `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`.\n   148\t\n   149\tThe receiver decodes, recomputes the numbering of the root and of every\n   150\tinstall child, re-emits and compares root ids\n   151\t(`:yin.k/undecodable :noncanonical-cursor-cells` on mismatch). In 6a's\n   152\t`decode*` the recomputation is the `emit` call (`rows.cljc:570-575`);\n   153\tthe **whole** of that call is wrapped so that only a lift-side\n   154\t`:yin.k/non-portable :unresolved-cursor-cells` (root or child) becomes\n   155\t`:yin.k/undecodable :unresolved-cursor-cells` with `:yin.k/path` prefixed\n   156\tby the carried root id (a child's path reads `[<root> 12 <name> …]`); a\n   157\tresource refusal propagates as itself; nothing else can arise there,\n   158\tsince step 5 has checked literals and markers. The validator order stays:\n   159\tnumbering (step 6) after step 5 and before `validate-frames`\n   160\t(`rows.cljc:576-578`; test `numbering-precedes-frame-defect`).\n   161\t\n   162\t### 1.5 Budgets and gates\n   163\t\n   164\t- `:cursor-cells`: the census size is charged first, before ranks,\n   165\t  counts or any walk (6a's gate at `rows.cljc:256`, kept).\n   166\t- `:depth`: the walk's nesting; a member step counts one level (6a's\n   167\t  gate at `rows.cljc:291-292`, kept, now in the walk).\n   168\t- `:work`: one unit per position visited (walk, key walk, cell scans,\n   169\t  count and rank preparation) or spelled, one unit per cell in a key\n   170\t  walk's memo key, and, **before every CBOR encoding made for a\n   171\t  comparison** (keys, rank labels, structural keys), the representation\n   172\t  bound of the value encoded, computed by reading it: 6a's `size` of\n   173\t  `check-label-bytes!` (`rows.cljc:218-229`), an upper bound of the\n   174\t  encoding's bytes. Spellings hold input literals by reference, so their\n   175\t  own size is their spelled positions. Hence every byte allocated for a\n   176\t  comparison is charged before the allocation, and the bytes alive at\n   177\t  any time are bounded by the budget plus the input. No polynomial bound\n   178\t  is claimed; the budget is the bound. Default `4194304`, the scale of\n   179\t  the deleted `:label-bytes` (appendix A: at most 624926 over 3300\n   180\t  generated states, 544554 with a 65536-character stream descriptor). A\n   181\t  literal of more than about 30000 scalars inside a guest set or map\n   182\t  exceeds it (6a allowed about 8000); in an ordered position a literal is\n   183\t  never spelled and costs one unit.\n   184\t- `:rows`, `:bytes`: unchanged. `:label-bytes` and `:search` are deleted.\n   185\t\n   186\tExceeding any budget is the resource refusal of carrier §10.10\n   187\t(`:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit [<key>]`), never a canonicality\n   188\tverdict, at lift and at decode alike.\n   189\t\n   190\t### 1.6 Totality\n   191\t\n   192\tThe root walk ends with an empty queue or a refusal. Before emission the\n   193\timplementation asserts that the numbering is a bijection from the\n   194\tcensus's cursor cells onto `1 … n`; a violation (a census cell no walk\n   195\treaches) is a defect, an exception without `:yin.k/status` carrying the\n   196\tmissing and extra cells, never a refusal and never a partially numbered\n   197\tcarrier. 6a's test `input-row-bodies-do-not-spend-label-bytes`\n   198\t(`rows_test.cljc:406-421`) declares exactly such a cell in `base` and\n   199\texpects `emit` to succeed (`:414`); §4 rewrites it.\n   200\t\n   201\t## 2. Proof of canonicality\n   202\t\n   203\tCall two configurations (abstract task, numbering, queue) **isomorphic**\n   204\twhen a bijection `π` of cursor cells carries one to the other up to the\n   205\thost order of every set, map and census, with `N'(π(c)) = N(c)` and\n   206\tqueues corresponding group by group. **Theorem:** on isomorphic\n   207\tconfigurations the procedure refuses at corresponding points or emits\n   208\tbyte-identical rows. The proof is an induction on the measure\n   209\t`(size of the walk's item as a sub-term, unnumbered cells of the walk)`.\n   210\t\n   211\t*Sort.* The key of a member is a function of the member and the\n   212\tnumbering up to isomorphism. The key walk reads structure, numbers,\n   213\tranks (a function of the census up to isomorphism) and occurrence counts\n   214\t(an isomorphism invariant); it orders nested containers by the keys of\n   215\ttheir members, canonical by the induction (a member is a proper sub-term,\n   216\tso the measure decreases); its only other listed-order input is the\n   217\torder in which it enters an interchangeable group, harmless by the tie\n   218\tstep below; and what it cannot certify it spells as `[:tie K … K]`, a\n   219\tfunction of `K` and the group's size. Equal keys therefore mean\n   220\tisomorphic members when the key is complete (a complete spelling is\n   221\tfaithful: structure slot by slot, every cell by local index, rank and\n   222\tcount, so matching indices is a bijection of the members' cells under\n   223\twhich the members agree).\n   224\t\n   225\t*Walk.* W1 and W2 read only table order and keys; W3 repeats W1 on the\n   226\tqueue in a canonical order. So the numbering before the tie test, the\n   227\tsurviving groups and their keys are functions of the configuration up to\n   228\tisomorphism, and so is the verdict of the test, whose inputs (key\n   229\tcompleteness, the sets of unnumbered cells of members) are structural.\n   230\t\n   231\t*Tie step.* Let `G` pass with members `e_1 … e_k`. Their keys are equal\n   232\tand complete, so for each pair there is an index-matched bijection\n   233\t`π_ij` of their unnumbered cells carrying `e_i` to `e_j`; the cells of\n   234\tdistinct members are disjoint (ii) and occur nowhere else in the state\n   235\t(iii), so the permutation composed from these bijections, extended by\n   236\tthe identity, is an automorphism of the whole state that permutes the\n   237\tmembers of `G` and fixes everything else. Entering `G` in two host orders\n   238\t`σ`, `σ'` gives configurations related by that automorphism: numbers\n   239\tagree by construction (the cells of the t-th entered member receive the\n   240\tsame block, by local index), structures agree, queues agree. The rest of\n   241\tthe procedure is equivariant by the theorem's induction, so the rows are\n   242\tthe same. Passing groups are mutually independent by (iii), so entering\n   243\tall of them before the next pass changes nothing.\n   244\t\n   245\t*Emission.* Cells are emitted by number only and every unordered slot is\n   246\tsorted by canonical bytes under the final numbering (carrier §5), so\n   247\tisomorphic final configurations emit the same rows. ∎\n   248\t\n   249\t*Termination.* There is no search and no re-entry: the only recursion is\n   250\tthe key walk, over a proper sub-term of its caller's item, so key walks\n   251\tnest at most as deep as the structure (`:depth`). Within one walk, every\n   252\tW3 pass except the last numbers a cell, and every tie step either numbers\n   253\ta cell or replaces the queued members by proper sub-terms of themselves\n   254\t(members with no unnumbered cell are equal values, which the carrier\n   255\trefuses as duplicates at §8 step 5). The measure decreases at each step\n   256\tand the budget bounds the count.\n   257\t\n   258\tConsequences: a function of structure, invariant under renaming and host\n   259\tenumeration (the theorem with `π` a renaming, a permutation of\n   260\tcontainers, or both); deterministic, so two lifts of one state are\n   261\tbyte-identical and the receiver's recomputation is well defined; sharing\n   262\tsurvives (one number per cell, every occurrence spells it); the\n   263\tcross-engine law of continuation §2.1 follows as before.\n   264\t\n   265\t## 3. What is refused\n   266\t\n   267\tRefused iff, when the root walk's last pass numbers nothing, some\n   268\tsurviving tie group fails §1.3. Concretely one of:\n   269\t\n   270\t- **(i) an incomplete key**: a tied member contains a container whose\n   271\t  own ties the key walk could not certify. `#{#{[A B] [B A]} #{[C D]\n   272\t  [D C]}}` (interchangeable; refused because equal residue keys need not\n   273\t  mean isomorphic members: `#{[A B] [B A]}` and `#{[A B] [C D]}` key\n   274\t  alike).\n   275\t- **(ii) two tied members share a cell**: the 2-cycle `#{[A B] [B A]}`,\n   276\t  the 3-cycle, the loop plus 2-cycle `#{[L L] [A B] [B A]}`\n   277\t  (`rows_test.cljc:312-329`, where `L` is separated by its key, `A B`\n   278\t  are not), the mixed-body `#{[A B] [A C]}` with `A` on another stream\n   279\t  position than `B, C` (all interchangeable and refused), and the\n   280\t  seven-cell triangle plus square (`rows_test.cljc:392-397`), which is\n   281\t  **not** interchangeable: a triangle edge entered first and a square\n   282\t  edge entered first give different rows, so no sound test can accept it\n   283\t  without a search.\n   284\t- **(iii) a tied member's cell occurs in a tied member of another\n   285\t  container**: `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`, `[#{#{X Y}} #{[X Z] [Y W]}]`, and the\n   286\t  reviewer's anchored state `[#{[A B] [A C] [B D] [C E] [D B] [E C]} A]`\n   287\t  (`A` is numbered by its ordered position, which does not break the tie\n   288\t  of the rest; all three interchangeable and refused).\n   289\t\n   290\tEqual bodies are not required (the mixed-body case) and an ordered anchor\n   291\tdoes not certify a whole component (the anchored case). 6a numbers the\n   292\t2-cycle, the 3-cycle, the loop plus 2-cycle, `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`, the\n   293\tmixed and the anchored states, and refuses the seven-cell state and the\n   294\ttwo-2-cycle state at `[:label-bytes]`. Discovery order numbers what 6a\n   295\trefuses at `[:label-bytes]` when the keys separate the members, and\n   296\tnumbers the path and `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]`, which 6a also numbers.\n   297\t\n   298\tMeasured (appendix A; refusals classified by entering the ties in every\n   299\tlisted order of every container when there are at most 720 such orders,\n   300\telse 48 sampled orders):\n   301\t\n   302\t| Generator | States | Numbered | Refused | of which interchangeable | not interchangeable | undecided |\n   303\t|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n   304\t| random values (1–6 cells, two bodies, nested sets, maps with cursor-ref keys, edge sets, sets of sets) | 2000 | 1981 | 19 | 16 | 3 | 0 |\n   305\t| frame-bearing (windows, K frames, environments, parked state) | 200 | 196 | 4 | 3 | 1 | 0 |\n   306\t| with install children | 100 | 98 | 2 | 1 | 0 | 1 |\n   307\t| reviewer's tie-heavy incidence families | 1000 | 832 | 168 | 92 | 17 | 59 |\n   308\t\n   309\tEvery refusal was (ii) or (iii); none was (i). Invariance failures and\n   310\tdecode failures: 0 of 3300. No production corpus exists yet (no\n   311\tversion-3 lift before 6b), so these are generator rates, not frequencies.\n   312\t\n   313\t**Fixtures.** Every state named in this section and in §1's example is a\n   314\tfixture of §5 (items 12–16), including the refused ones.\n   315\t\n   316\t## 4. Replacing 6a: deleted, rewritten, kept\n   317\t\n   318\tAgainst `srvm-phase6a` (`d77da9f0`), `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` and\n   319\t`test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc` (line numbers of those files).\n   320\t\n   321\t**rows.cljc, deleted.** `check-label-bytes!` (`:212-230`) as a bound on\n   322\tlabels (its `size` function, `:218-229`, survives as the representation\n   323\tbound of §1.5); inside `canonical-numbering` (`:236-355`): the label\n   324\tseeding (`:252-255`), `ce-slot`/`ce` (`:257-284`), `positions`\n   325\t(`:285-325`) except its depth gate, `classes` (`:326-330`), `refine`\n   326\t(`:331-337`), `search` (`:338-354`), the `checked-*` wrappers\n   327\t(`:245-251`); in `default-budget` (`:200-202`) the keys `:label-bytes`\n   328\tand `:search`.\n   329\t\n   330\t**rows.cljc, rewritten.**\n   331\t- `canonical-numbering` (`:236-355`), same signature and contract\n   332\t  (`task`, optional `budget` → `{source-id :yin.k/cc-n}`; empty census\n   333\t  → `{}`): the census gate (`:256`) first; body ranks and occurrence\n   334\t  counts; then the root walk of §1.2 with its queue, the tie test, the\n   335\t  refusal and the totality assertion, as in the prototype's sections\n   336\t  \"cell scans\", \"runs\", \"spelling\" and \"procedure\"\n   337\t  (`collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj:79-416`), charging as\n   338\t  §1.5 says. Portable `.cljc` per continuation §12: no `@#'` reflection;\n   339\t  `mapv`/`into`/`reduce`, not `for`, over sequences that may exceed 32\n   340\t  elements (the prototype's `group-failure` uses `for`: replace);\n   341\t  `(assoc {} …)` never `(assoc nil …)`; atoms are fine on all three\n   342\t  hosts. 6a's `bytes-key` (`:91-94`) takes encoded bytes; the prototype's\n   343\t  takes a value: keep 6a's and encode first.\n   344\t- `descriptors` (`:117-124`): reused, with the synthetic `:entry` row\n   345\t  added (`[:V :V]`).\n   346\t- `default-budget` (`:200-202`): `:work` default `4194304`.\n   347\t- `emit-node` (`:181-197`): thread a row path so a child's refusal\n   348\t  reports `[12 <install name> …]`.\n   349\t- `decode*` recompute (`:570-575`): the translation of §1.4 around the\n   350\t  whole `emit` call.\n   351\t- Namespace docstring (`:1-17`): \"canonical cursor-cell numbering\" →\n   352\t  \"discovery-order cursor-cell numbering (design note §1)\".\n   353\t\n   354\t**rows.cljc, kept unchanged.** `row-table`, `pending-table`, `node`,\n   355\t`refuse!`/`bad!`, the marker checks, `bytes-key`/`compare-bytes`/\n   356\t`byte-compare`/`sorted-bytes`, the census order (`:137-156`),\n   357\t`emit-slot`/`emit-node` apart from the path, `spend!` (`:205-209`),\n   358\t`emit-numbered` (`:358-363`), `emit` (`:366-372`), `structural?`,\n   359\t`literal?`, `check-abstract!`, all of `decode*` before `:570` and after\n   360\t`:575`, `decode`, `canonical-under`, `frame->rows`, `rows->frame`,\n   361\t`validate-frames`, and the `:bytes`, `:rows`, `:depth`, `:cursor-cells`\n   362\tkeys with their defaults.\n   363\t\n   364\t**rows_test.cljc, kept.** `task-node`, `cursor-cell-body`, `fixture`\n   365\t(`:16-37`), `portable-frame` and `bijection`, `row-ids-are-jing-default`,\n   366\t`replace-row`, `window-sorted-and-unique`,\n   367\t`canonical-under-is-renaming-invariant` (`:143-156`),\n   368\t`alias-topology-and-census` (`:159-168`), `gates-and-graph-before-hashes`,\n   369\t`numbering-precedes-frame-defect` (`:206-213`),\n   370\t`schema-and-literal-canonicality`, `child-scope-and-shared-child-dag`\n   371\t(`:240-249`), `pending-envelope-and-nil-literal-are-preserved`,\n   372\t`task-root-checks-carried-code-and-frame`, `reversed` and\n   373\t`unordered-containers-are-order-free` (`:285-304`),\n   374\t`census-is-numeric-from-ten-cursor-cells` (`:331-338`), the marker tests\n   375\t(`:341-381`), `cursor-cell-and-identity-shaped-literal-are-distinct`\n   376\t(`:384-389`), the fixture `seven-cursor-cell-triangle-square`\n   377\t(`:392-397`).\n   378\t\n   379\t**rows_test.cljc, rewritten.**\n   380\t- `refinement-search-on-symmetric-cursor-cells-is-bounded` (`:171-178`)\n   381\t  → `symmetric-cursor-cells-are-interchangeable`: the two assertions on\n   382\t  equal roots and two numbers stay; the `{:search 1}` assertions become\n   383\t  `{:work 1}` → `:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit [:work]`.\n   384\t- `canonical-roots-are-pinned` (`:307-328`): `fab223…` and `c4a7c7…`\n   385\t  stay; the loop plus 2-cycle block (`:312-328`) asserts the\n   386\t  `:unresolved-cursor-cells` refusal with path `[15]` and cells `[:a :b]`\n   387\t  for the task and the same verdict and path for `renamed`; the\n   388\t  `{:search 2}` assertion and the pin `55ff168e…` go.\n   389\t- `intermediate-label-bytes-are-bounded` (`:400-403`) →\n   390\t  `seven-cursor-cells-are-refused`: `:yin.k/non-portable\n   391\t  :unresolved-cursor-cells`, path `[15]`, cells `[:a … :g]`, and the\n   392\t  renamed and shuffled state gives the same verdict and path.\n   393\t- `input-row-bodies-do-not-spend-label-bytes` (`:406-421`) →\n   394\t  `large-literals-in-ordered-positions-are-free`: `base` (its census\n   395\t  cell `:x` is unreachable) now asserts the totality defect of §1.6 (an\n   396\t  exception whose data is `{:missing [:x] :extra []}` and has no\n   397\t  `:yin.k/status`) in place of `(is (nil? (error #(rows/emit base))))`\n   398\t  (`:414`); `reachable` and `custody` keep emitting and decoding to the\n   399\t  expected tasks (`:415-421`); add: the same 400000-element literal\n   400\t  inside a guest set refuses `:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit\n   401\t  [:work]` under the default budget.\n   402\t\n   403\t## 5. Acceptance for the rewritten 6a numbering\n   404\t\n   405\t12. `rows-test/discovery-order-numbering`: `canonical-numbering` returns\n   406\t    the maps of appendix A for `fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y`, the review\n   407\t    counterexample, `[x x y]`, `[x y y]`, `[#{X Y} X]`, the witness\n   408\t    `[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]`, `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]`, the path and the two\n   409\t    frame fixtures.\n   410\t13. `rows-test/occurrence-count-separates-members`: the review\n   411\t    counterexample numbers `Y cc-1, X cc-2`, pinned `2d9873a8…` (6a's\n   412\t    root); the path pinned `726a9fbb…`; `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]` pinned\n   413\t    `d84480d4…`; each equal after renaming plus reversal of every set.\n   414\t14. `rows-test/interchangeable-ties`: `#{X Y}`, `#{[X X] [Y Y]}`,\n   415\t    `{X 1, Y 1}`, `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` number without refusal; reversal and\n   416\t    renaming give equal roots; pins `07eda35d…`, `da69c679…`,\n   417\t    `49d4478c…`, `18b70de6…`.\n   418\t15. `rows-test/alias-pattern-is-part-of-the-key`: `#{[X X] [Y Z]}` and\n   419\t    `{X X, Y Z}` give one root for both enumerations and after renaming,\n   420\t    pinned `387f294a…` and `be62ba43…` (6a's roots); the witness numbers\n   421\t    `B cc-1, A cc-2`, pinned `0193312…` (6a's root), and decodes.\n   422\t16. `rows-test/unresolved-cursor-cells-refuse`: the 2-cycle, the\n   423\t    3-cycle, the loop plus 2-cycle, the seven-cell state,\n   424\t    `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`, the mixed-body, the anchored and the two-2-cycle\n   425\t    states refuse at `emit` with `:yin.k/non-portable`\n   426\t    `:unresolved-cursor-cells`, the paths and cells of appendix A, the\n   427\t    same verdict and path after renaming plus shuffling; a carrier of\n   428\t    each built with `emit-numbered` decodes to `:yin.k/undecodable\n   429\t    :unresolved-cursor-cells` with the path prefixed by the root, never\n   430\t    `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`; a parent whose install `'a` holds the\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
